window.MANUAL_INDEX=[{"p":"All-settings.html","t":"All settings A–Z","h":"All settings A–Z","a":"","x":"All settings A–Z Every setting by its console name, with the menu page it is on. Type <NAME> <value> on the console to set one. Console name Setting Menu page Default Values ADIF_UDP ADIF over UDP Logging programs (empty) text AGC AGC Receive SLOW OFF, SLOW, MED, FAST ANF Auto notch Receive OFF ON, OFF APF CW peak filter CW OFF ON, OFF ATT Attenuator Receive OFF OFF, 6, 12, 18 AUDIO Volume Receive 20 0–100 BATT Battery protection Power & battery OFF OFF, LIFEPO4, AGM BATT_SHUT Shut down when flat Power & battery OFF ON, OFF BFO Birdie shift Calibration 0 -3000–3000 BRIDGE SWR bridge Calibration 100 25–400 BW Filter width Receive per mode 50–5000 CESSB CESSB Transmit OFF ON, OFF CLUSTER Cluster connection Station OFF ON, OFF CLUSTER_AGE Spot age Station 30 5–120 CLUSTER_HOST Cluster node address Station ve7cc.net:23 text COMP Compressor Transmit 0 0–10 CONSOLE Console Network ON ON, OFF CTCSS_RX CTCSS receive Receive OFF OFF or one of the 38 standard tones (67.0–250.3 Hz) CTCSS_TX CTCSS transmit Transmit OFF OFF or one of the 38 standard tones (67.0–250.3 Hz) CW_DELAY Break-in delay CW 300 50–3000 CW_INPUT Key type CW STRAIGHT STRAIGHT, IAMBIC, IAMBICB, ULTIMAT, BUG CWAUTOSP Autospace CW OFF ON, OFF CWDEC CW decoder CW ON ON, OFF CWLSPACE Letter space CW 3 3–9 CWM1 Macro 1 CW CQ|CQ CQ CQ DE {MYCALL} {MYCALL} K text CWM2 Macro 2 CW ANS|{CALL} DE {MYCALL} {MYCALL} K text CWM3 Macro 3 CW RPT|{CALL} TU UR {RST} {RST} 73 DE {MYCALL} K text CWM4 Macro 4 CW TU|{CALL} TU 73 DE {MYCALL} SK text CWM5 Macro 5 CW QRZ|QRZ? DE {MYCALL} K text CWM6 Macro 6 CW AGN|AGN? K text CWM7 Macro 7 CW GRID|{CALL} GRID {MYGRID} {MYGRID} K text CWM8 Macro 8 CW MYCALL|{MYCALL} text CWRATIO Dash length CW 3.0 2.5, 2.7, 3.0, 3.3, 3.5, 4.0, 4.5 CWREV Reverse paddles CW OFF ON, OFF CWWEIGHT Weight CW 50 25–75 CWWSPACE Word space CW 7 5–21 DRIVE Drive Transmit 40 1–100 ePTT External PTT Transmit OFF ON, OFF FDVR FreeDV Reporter FT8 and digital OFF ON, OFF FDVR_HOST Reporter server FT8 and digital qso.freedv.org text FDVR_MSG Reporter message FT8 and digital (empty) text FOLLOW Speaker follows TCI Network ON ON, OFF FT8_AUTO FT8 automation FT8 and digital ANS OFF, ANS, CQRESP FT8_CQ CQ word FT8 and digital (empty) text FT8_CQEVERY CQ every FT8 and digital 1 1–4 FT8_PREFER Hunt prefers FT8 and digital (empty) text FT8_RECENT Worked recently FT8 and digital 24 0–720 FT8_SKIP Hunt skips FT8 and digital BAND NONE, BAND, ANY FT8_TXF FT8/FT4/FT2 TX audio FT8 and digital 1500 200–3000 FT8_WDOG TX watchdog FT8 and digital 10 0–60 FTXLOCK Lock tuning in FT modes FT8 and digital ON ON, OFF GPS GPS receiver GPS ON ON, OFF GPS_DELAY GPS delay GPS 100 0–1000 GPS_GRID Grid from GPS GPS OFF AUTO, OFF GPS_HOST gpsd address GPS 127.0.0.1:2947 text GPS_TIME Set the clock from GPS GPS AUTO AUTO, OFF HAMLIB Hamlib (rigctld) Network ON ON, OFF HIGHCUT High cut Receive per mode 50–5000 HPSDR HPSDR server Network ON ON, OFF HPSDR_IQINV HPSDR swap I/Q Network OFF ON, OFF HPSDR_LNA HPSDR LNA to IF gain Network OFF ON, OFF HSMIC Microphone (headset) Headset & USB audio OFF OFF or a PipeWire input HSMICGAIN Headset mic gain Headset & USB audio 0 −20–30 dB IF IF gain Receive 60 0–100 IFAUTO Auto IF gain Receive OFF ON, OFF JS8_API JS8Call API FT8 and digital 127.0.0.1:2442 text JS8_STALE JS8 entries kept FT8 and digital 30 0–1440 JS8_TXF JS8 offset FT8 and digital 1500 100–2900 JTTY_TXF JTTY TX audio FT8 and digital 1500 200–3000 KEYER Keyer CW AUTO AUTO, ON, OFF KNOBS Knobs Tuning and knobs AUTO AUTO, LOCAL, MIDI LICENSE US license class Band plan OFF OFF, NOVICE, TECHNICIAN, GENERAL, ADVANCED, EXTRA LOCALPTT Mic PTT Transmit ON ON, OFF LOWCUT Low cut Receive per mode 0–4900 MIC Mic gain Transmit 30 0–50 MIDI MIDI Tuning and knobs ON ON, OFF MODEQRG Tune to mode frequency FT8 and digital ON ON, OFF MYCALL My call sign Station (empty) text MYGRID My grid Station (empty) text NB Noise blanker Receive 0 0–10 NR Noise reduction Receive 0 0–10 NRMODE Noise reduction type Receive SPEC SPEC, LMS"},{"p":"Band-plan.html","t":"Band plan","h":"Band plan","a":"","x":"Band plan More → Band plan sets where the radio may transmit, and shows your US license privileges on the scope."},{"p":"Band-plan.html","t":"Band plan","h":"Transmit band plan","a":"transmit-band-plan","x":"Choose your country, or your IARU region. The radio then refuses to transmit unless the whole signal (the dial plus the filter or tone offset) fits inside an allowed range. The header shows TX blocked when you are outside, and the radio will not key, whatever asks it to: PTT, CAT, TCI, HPSDR, the keyer or the digital modes. Plan Covers Off no limit (the default) USA, Canada, UK, Australia, Japan, New Zealand that country's amateur bands IARU Region 1, 2, 3 the IARU region's bands, with the WRC-15 60 m band Custom the ranges you type in Custom ranges Under the buttons the page lists the ranges of the selected plan in kHz. Copy these to Custom copies them into Custom ranges and selects Custom, so you can start from your country's plan and change it. The presets follow each country's rules as of November 2026. 60 m is often by permit or a separate license. Your license is what counts : if it differs from the preset, copy the plan to Custom and change it."},{"p":"Band-plan.html","t":"Band plan","h":"Custom ranges","a":"custom-ranges","x":"Type kHz ranges as low-high , separated by spaces or commas, for example: 3500-3800 5351.5-5366.5 7000-7200 14000-14350 Add any other ranges your license allows (MARS, CAP, 11 m where permitted). With a transverter, the 10 m IF is not checked."},{"p":"Band-plan.html","t":"Band plan","h":"US license class and privilege ribbons","a":"us-license-class-and-privilege-ribbons","x":"US license class (Novice, Technician, General, Advanced, Extra) outlines your row in the colored ribbons along the top of the scope and warns in the header when the signal you would transmit in the current mode falls outside your privileges or in the wrong segment. The ribbons show the FCC §97.301 and §97.305 privileges: CW only, CW and data, CW and phone, plus the 60 m channels. The license class is a guide only: it does not stop the transmitter (the transmit band plan does that). More → Display → Band privilege ribbons chooses All classes, My class or Off."},{"p":"Band-plan.html","t":"Band plan","h":"Settings","a":"settings","x":"Setting Console name Default Values What it does US license class LICENSE OFF OFF, NOVICE, TECHNICIAN, GENERAL, ADVANCED, EXTRA Shows your band privileges on the scope and warns outside them Transmit band plan TXPLAN OFF OFF, US, CANADA, UK, AUSTRALIA, JAPAN, NZ, REGION1, REGION2, REGION3, CUSTOM The radio will not transmit outside these amateur bands (the whole signal must fit). Off: no limit. Pick your country, or your IARU region; check it against your license Custom ranges TXEDGES (empty) text For Custom: the kHz ranges you may transmit in, e.g. 3500-3800 5351.5-5366.5 7000-7200. Add any others your license allows (MARS, CAP, 11 m where permitted)"},{"p":"CW-voice-keyer-and-recordings.html","t":"CW, voice keyer and recordings","h":"CW, voice keyer and recordings","a":"","x":"CW, voice keyer and recordings"},{"p":"CW-voice-keyer-and-recordings.html","t":"CW, voice keyer and recordings","h":"CW","a":"cw","x":"Choose CW (or CWR , the opposite sideband) from the mode sheet. The CW panel opens above the control strip."},{"p":"CW-voice-keyer-and-recordings.html","t":"CW, voice keyer and recordings","h":"The keyer","a":"the-keyer","x":"Plug a straight key, bug or paddles into the key jack (tip = dash or straight key, ring = dot) and set More → CW → Key type : Key type For STRAIGHT a straight key, or an external keyer IAMBIC, IAMBICB paddles, iambic mode A or B ULTIMAT paddles, the last paddle pressed wins BUG a semi-automatic bug: dots from the dot paddle, dashes by hand Speed (WPM), Pitch (the tone and where the CW signal sits), Sidetone (level, independent of the volume) and Break-in delay (how long after the last element the radio returns to receive) complete it. Reverse paddles swaps dot and dash for left-handed operators."},{"p":"CW-voice-keyer-and-recordings.html","t":"CW, voice keyer and recordings","h":"Keyer timing","a":"keyer-timing","x":"Under More → CW → Timing : Setting Standard What it does Weight 50 How much of each element and the space after it is key-down. Above 50 the dots and dashes get longer and the spaces shorter (a heavier sound); below 50, lighter. The speed stays the same. Dash length 3.0 A dash in dots, from 2.5 to 4.5. Letter space 3 The space between letters sent from Type… and macros, in dots. Word space 7 The space between words from Type… and macros, in dots. Larger letter and word spaces at a normal speed give Farnsworth-style practice. Autospace OFF Paddles: when you pause after an element, the keyer waits out a full letter space before the next one, so letters don't run together. A paddle you touch during that wait is remembered and sent. Keyer decides when the jack drives the keyer: AUTO (default) in the CW modes, ON always (for CW through an HPSDR client), OFF never (the jack is then a plain PTT line)."},{"p":"CW-voice-keyer-and-recordings.html","t":"CW, voice keyer and recordings","h":"Decoder","a":"decoder","x":"The panel shows the decoded text and the estimated speed. Tune the station onto the dial marker (tap the signal on the scope). The decoder also finds a signal up to about 250 Hz off the pitch and follows it as it drifts; the panel then says how far off it is (\"following +60 Hz\"), and you can zero beat to center it. It follows speeds from about 5 to 55 WPM, and it does not print noise when no one is sending. Weak signals: the decoder listens through several filters at once, each matched to a range of speeds (a narrower filter takes out more noise at slower speeds), and shows the text from the one whose timing fits Morse best. In tests with computer-generated CW it copies at 20 WPM down to about 6 dB below the noise in a 2500 Hz bandwidth, and keeps up with moderate fading. Decoder on turns it off and on ( CWDEC ). CW peak filter ( APF ) adds 6 dB at the pitch, about 100 Hz wide."},{"p":"CW-voice-keyer-and-recordings.html","t":"CW, voice keyer and recordings","h":"Zero beat","a":"zero-beat","x":"Next to the speed, the zero-beat indicator shows how far the strongest steady tone in the receive filter is from your pitch, which is how far that station is from your transmit frequency. The needle sits in the middle and turns green with \"0 beat\" within ±10 Hz; amber within ±50 Hz. The scale is finer near the middle and reaches ±300 Hz. Tap the indicator to tune onto the station (console zerobeat ), so you answer on its frequency. It works with the decoder off, and shows \"—\" when there is no steady signal, or while you transmit. It reads the signal you hear: with several in the filter, narrow the filter or tap the one you want on the scope first."},{"p":"CW-voice-keyer-and-recordings.html","t":"CW, voice keyer and recordings","h":"Macros","a":"macros","x":"Below the text: Call and RST of the station you are working, Type… to send any text, eight macro buttons and Stop . A macro is LABEL|text . {MYCALL} , {MYGRID} , {CALL} and {RST} are filled in when it is sent. Press and hold a macro button to edit it, or edit them in More → CW → Macros . Text is sent by the keyer at the keyer speed. Console: cwtext <text> , cwstop . Keyer Setting Console name Default Values What it does Speed WPM 12 5–50 Keyer speed, words per minute Pitch PITCH 700 100–3000 CW tone, Hz Sidetone SIDETONE 25 0–100 Sidetone level (not affected by volume) Break-in delay CW_DELAY 300 50–3000 Back to receive after this many ms Key type CW_INPUT STRAIGHT STRAIGHT, IAMBIC, IAMBICB, ULTIMAT, BUG Straight key, bug or paddles Reverse paddles CWREV OFF ON, OFF Swap the dot and dash paddles (for left-handed operators) Keyer KEYER AUTO AUTO, ON, OFF AUTO = in CW modes Timing Setting Console name Default Values What it does Weight CWWEIGHT 50 25–75 Key-down share of each element and the space after it: 50 = standard, more = heavier Dash length CWRATIO 3.0 2.5, 2.7, 3.0, 3.3, 3.5, 4.0, 4.5 A dash in dots (3.0 = standard) Letter space CWLSPACE 3 3–9 Space between letters sent from text and macros, in dots (3 = standard) Word space CWWSPACE 7 5–21 Space between words sent from text and macros, in dots (7 = standard) Autospace CWAUTOSP OFF ON, OFF Paddles: when you pause after an element, wait out a full letter space before the next Receive Setting Console name Default Values What it does CW decoder CWDEC ON ON, OFF Decode CW on the panel above the controls CW peak filter APF OFF ON, OFF +6 dB at the CW pitch Macros Setting Console name Default Values What it does Macro 1 CWM1 CQ|CQ CQ CQ DE {MYCALL} {MYCALL} K text LABEL|text; {CALL} {RST} {MYCALL} {MYGRID} Macro 2 CWM2 ANS|{CALL} DE {MYCALL} {MYCALL} K text Same as macro 1 Macro 3 CWM3 RPT|{CALL} TU UR {RST} {RST} 73 DE {MYCALL} K text Same as macro 1 Macro 4 CWM4 TU|{CALL} TU 73 DE {MYCALL} SK text Same as macro 1 Macro 5 CWM5 QRZ|QRZ? DE {MYCALL} K text Same as macro 1 Macro 6 CWM6 AGN|AGN? K text Same as macro 1 Macro 7 CWM7 GRID|{CALL} GRID {MYGRID} {MYGRID} K text Same as macro 1 Macro 8 CWM8 MYCALL|{MYCALL} text Same as macro 1"},{"p":"CW-voice-keyer-and-recordings.html","t":"CW, voice keyer and recordings","h":"Voice keyer","a":"voice-keyer","x":"Eight voice messages kept on the radio. Open them from the Voice keyer tile in SSB, AM and FM. Tap a message to transmit it in place of the microphone (mic gain, the compressor and the transmit equalizer still apply). Repeat sends it again every Voice keyer repeat seconds until Stop , PTT or any other transmission (handy for calling CQ). Edit records a message from the radio's microphone, or from this browser's microphone over HTTPS; you can listen to it on the radio's speaker or in the browser, rename it or delete it. Recordings are trimmed and leveled; each can be up to 60 s. Console: vk play N [repeat] , vk rec N , vk stop , vk delete N . Setting Console name Default Values What it does Voice keyer level VKLEVEL 100 0–200 Transmit level of the voice messages, % (100 = as recorded) Voice keyer repeat VKREPEAT 6 1–60 Seconds between repeats when Repeat is on Messages Setting Console name Default Values What it does Message 1 label VKM1 CQ text Button label for voice message 1 Message 2 label VKM2 QRZ text Button label for voice message 2 Message 3 label VKM3 73 text Button label for voice message 3 Message 4 label VKM4 MSG 4 text Button label for voice message 4 Message 5 label VKM5 MSG 5 text Button label for voice message 5 Message 6 label VKM6 MSG 6 text Button label for voice message 6 Message 7 label VKM7 MSG 7 text Button label for voice message 7 Message 8 label VKM8 MSG 8 text Button label for voice message 8"},{"p":"CW-voice-keyer-and-recordings.html","t":"CW, voice keyer and recordings","h":"Recordings","a":"recordings","x":"More → Recordings (or ● Record on the Quick page ) records what you hear (after the filter, NR and EQ) and your microphone while you transmit, to the radio's SD card as WAV files named by UTC time, frequency and mode. The header shows ● REC 1:23 while recording. The list plays a recording in the browser, downloads it or deletes it. A recording stops by itself after 2 hours, or when less than 200 MB is free. Console: rec start|stop|list|delete <name> ."},{"p":"Clock-and-band-conditions.html","t":"Clock and band conditions","h":"Clock and band conditions","a":"","x":"Clock and band conditions More → Clock & conditions has two tools: one checks and sets the clocks against network time, and one shows the current HF band conditions. The band conditions also have a small indicator in the header."},{"p":"Clock-and-band-conditions.html","t":"Clock and band conditions","h":"Band conditions","a":"band-conditions","x":"The indicator sits next to the clock. The top line is the solar flux (SFI). The second line is the K index and the MUF (maximum usable frequency) in MHz. The dot shows how the band you are tuned to is doing right now: green is Good, amber is Fair, red is Poor. An empty dot means you are outside 80–10 m. The K index turns amber at 4 and red at 5 or more (a geomagnetic storm). The indicator dims when the data is more than 6 hours old. On a phone it shows only the dot and the solar flux. Tap the indicator for the details: sunspots, A index, X-ray, foF2, the geomagnetic field, the expected noise level, and a day and night table for 80-40, 30-20, 17-15 and 12-10 m. Your band is marked with ▸, and the column for the current time is brighter. Day or night is worked out from where you are (the GPS fix, or My grid ), not from the time zone of the device you are using. Refresh fetches the data again. The data comes from N0NBH's solar feed (hamqsl.com). The radio fetches it once an hour, or 10 minutes after a failed try, and only while Band conditions is ON. The radio needs internet for this; your browser does not. If the radio can't reach the feed, the indicator stays hidden, and More → Clock & conditions says why. Setting Console name Default Values What it does Band conditions SOLAR ON ON, OFF Solar flux, K index and MUF in the header, and HF conditions by day and night"},{"p":"Clock-and-band-conditions.html","t":"Clock and band conditions","h":"Clock tool","a":"clock-tool","x":"FT8 and the other timed modes need the clock right to within about a second, and a tenth of a second is better. When the Pi is online it keeps network time by itself (systemd-timesyncd). The clock tool lets you see how good the time is, set it right away, and keep the real-time clock (RTC) right for when you have no internet. The Pi 5 has an RTC built in; it keeps time while the Pi is off once you connect its battery. A Pi 4 needs an RTC add-on board. The page shows: Network time : whether the Pi says it is synchronized. This Pi's clock : how far the clock is from the time server, measured directly with a few NTP packets. The fastest reply is used. Real-time clock : how far the RTC is from the Pi's clock, measured to about 10 ms. The RTC itself only counts whole seconds. Last : the result of the last action. The buttons: Button What it does Check now asks the time server; changes nothing Set Pi clock from network moves the Pi's clock to network time now Write Pi clock to RTC sets the RTC from the Pi's clock (do this when the Pi's clock is right) Set Pi clock from RTC takes the time from the RTC; for no internet and no GPS Sync all network time → the Pi's clock → the RTC Setting a clock is not allowed while you are transmitting. A clock that jumps during an FT8 transmission would put it out of step. Out in the field, a GPS receiver sets the clock too. The clock tool and the GPS use the same helper, /opt/jeSDR/jesdr-settime , which the package's sudo rule allows. No other root access is needed. Setting Console name Default Values What it does Network time server NTP_SERVER pool.ntp.org text host or host:port; your router or a local server works too"},{"p":"Clock-and-band-conditions.html","t":"Clock and band conditions","h":"If it doesn't work","a":"if-it-doesnt-work","x":"\"did not answer (UDP 123 blocked or no internet?)\" : the network blocks NTP. Try your router's address as the server. \"refused (rate limit)\" : the server is asking you to slow down. Wait a minute and try again. \"RTC: none found\" : there is no /dev/rtc0 . A Pi 4 needs an RTC board and its overlay in /boot/firmware/config.txt . On an sBitx whose clock vanished after the I2C change, see Troubleshooting . \"holds no valid time\" : the RTC lost power, so its battery may be flat. Set the Pi's clock first, then Write Pi clock to RTC . \"sudo refused\" or \"not installed\" : reinstall the jeSDR package."},{"p":"Clock-and-band-conditions.html","t":"Clock and band conditions","h":"Console","a":"console","x":"solar the band conditions, one line plus the band table solar refresh fetch them now solar on|off the SOLAR setting clock the clock status (network time, the Pi's clock, the RTC) clock check ask the time server; changes nothing clock set set the Pi's clock from network time clock rtcwrite set the RTC from the Pi's clock clock rtcread set the Pi's clock from the RTC clock sync network time -> the Pi's clock -> the RTC"},{"p":"Connecting-other-programs.html","t":"Connecting other programs","h":"Connecting other programs","a":"","x":"Connecting other programs The radio serves itself to other programs on the Pi and on your network: Interface Port Use it for Hamlib rigctld TCP 4532 WSJT-X, fldigi, JS8Call, loggers (rig Hamlib NET rigctl ) TCI (WebSocket) TCP 50001 SDROxide, loggers, TCI-aware apps: IQ to the program, TX audio modulated by the radio, two-way control HPSDR Protocol 1 (Hermes-Lite 2) UDP 1024 SDROxide, SparkSDR, SDR Console: the program does the DSP and sends TX IQ Web UI TCP 8443 (HTTPS), 8080 the radio's screen in any browser Console TCP 8081 set-up and status ( Console commands ) Audio devices PipeWire: jeSDR Receive , jeSDR Transmit , jeSDR Speaker the radio's audio for programs on the Pi ( below ) MIDI ALSA jeSDR:Controls the knobs, key and PTT ( MIDI ) WSJT-X UDP , ADIF UDP your choice GridTracker, JTAlert, Log4OM, N1MM+"},{"p":"Connecting-other-programs.html","t":"Connecting other programs","h":"WSJT-X, fldigi and other Hamlib programs","a":"wsjt-x-fldigi-and-other-hamlib-programs","x":"Choose DIGI on the radio (or let the program switch it: asking for PKTUSB selects DIGI). In WSJT-X: Settings → Radio : rig Hamlib NET rigctl , network server 127.0.0.1:4532 (or the radio's address from another computer), PTT method CAT . Settings → Audio : input jeSDR Receive , output jeSDR Transmit (see below ). Frequency, mode, PTT, split, the tuning step, volume, IF gain, squelch, NR, NB, drive, mic gain, keyer speed, compressor, monitor and AGC all work both ways, and changes show up at once on the radio's screen. The S-meter, power and SWR can be read; CW text can be sent with b / \\send_morse . l ? and u ? list the supported levels and functions. JS8Call is set up the same way (rig Hamlib NET rigctl , PTT CAT, audio jeSDR Receive / jeSDR Transmit), but choose JS8 on the radio rather than DIGI: jeSDR then also reads JS8Call through its TCP API and shows its traffic in the JS8 panel. See JS8 ."},{"p":"Connecting-other-programs.html","t":"Connecting other programs","h":"Audio for programs on the Pi","a":"audio-for-programs-on-the-pi","x":"jeSDR gives programs on the Pi audio devices of their own, through PipeWire: Device Kind What it is jeSDR Receive input (a microphone, to the program) what the radio hears: 48 kHz mono, before the noise reduction and the speaker's equalizer jeSDR Transmit output (a speaker, to the program) what the radio sends when it transmits in DIGI or JS8 jeSDR Speaker output played on the radio's speaker (and the headset's headphones), at the volume knob's level; the desktop's default output Programs that use PulseAudio or PipeWire (WSJT-X, JS8Call, fldigi, Qt and GTK programs) list them by these names; ALSA-only programs reach them through the pipewire (or pulse ) ALSA device. No device numbers to match, and plugging in a USB device does not move them. (jeSDR has no ALSA loopback cards any more.) The audio level into the radio is the program's own output level (and the radio's Drive ): keep the program's level so that the ALC meter stays low. The radio's codec itself is hidden from the desktop (a WirePlumber rule in the package): it carries the radio's I/Q signal, not sound to play. The package keeps the radio user's PipeWire running from boot ( loginctl enable-linger ), so the devices are there before anyone logs in. jeSDR Speaker asks to be the desktop's default output, so a program that just plays to \"the speaker\" is heard on the radio. While a program plays into it, the speaker plays that program instead of the radio's own receiver; when it stops, the receiver comes back. The speaker stays quiet while transmitting, as always. jeSDR Receive asks to be the desktop's default input, so a program set to the default input hears the radio. jeSDR Transmit is never the default: choose it by name in WSJT-X, JS8Call and the like. If you once chose another input or output in the desktop's volume menu, the desktop keeps that choice: pick the jeSDR device there (or in the program) instead. status on the console shows whether a program is using each device. On Raspberry Pi OS Lite the package installs PipeWire; programs there (fldigi run headless, for example) use the same devices. Updating removes the loopback set-up older installers added ( /etc/modprobe.d/jesdr-aloop.conf ); the loopback cards disappear at the next reboot."},{"p":"Connecting-other-programs.html","t":"Connecting other programs","h":"SDROxide","a":"sdroxide","x":"TCI: in SDROxide Radio → TCI , server <radio>:50001 , IQ rate 48 or 96 kHz. SDROxide sends TX audio and the radio modulates it in the selected mode; mode, filter, NB/NR, notch, AGC, squelch, volume, lock, keyer speed, CW macros, monitor, tune drive, RIT, XIT and split work both ways. For CW use the key jack. HPSDR: Radio → HPSDR → Discover ; the radio appears as a Hermes-Lite 2 . Use 48 or 96 kHz and leave Swap I/Q on (if the spectrum is upside down, turn it off in SDROxide or set HPSDR swap I/Q , not both). SDROxide modulates and sends IQ; full scale is the radio's calibrated full power at Drive 100. Hearing SDROxide on the radio's speaker: SDROxide demodulates for itself and plays to the Pi's default output, which is jeSDR Speaker : its audio comes out of the radio's speaker, at the radio's volume knob, in place of the radio's own receiver while SDROxide plays. If it plays elsewhere, choose jeSDR Speaker as SDROxide's audio output (or in the desktop's volume menu). With a TCI client the radio's mode follows the client's: USB, LSB, CW, AM, FM and DIGL switch the radio. DIGU keeps the radio's own digital mode (FT8, FT4, FT2, JTTY, RTTY, PSK, RADE, DIGI) and otherwise chooses DIGI; in JS8 the radio stays in JS8 (JS8Call asks for USB). With Speaker follows TCI the radio's own receiver tunes to where you click in the client's waterfall (what you hear when SDROxide is not playing). Speaker follows TCI ( FOLLOW , ON) is under More → Network → TCI : Setting Console name Default Values What it does Speaker follows TCI FOLLOW ON ON, OFF Listen where the TCI client is tuned"},{"p":"Connecting-other-programs.html","t":"Connecting other programs","h":"Browser audio","a":"browser-audio","x":"The 🔈 button next to PTT plays the radio's receive audio in the browser (16 kHz, about 32 kB/s, after the noise reduction and equalizer). On another device it is on by default and starts at the first tap; on the radio's own screen it is hidden. Listening in this browser sets this device's volume and the delay: Low (80 ms), Normal (150 ms) or Steady (300 ms) for a poor Wi-Fi link. Nothing is sent while transmitting."},{"p":"Connecting-other-programs.html","t":"Connecting other programs","h":"Network","a":"network","x":"More → Network turns each server on or off and sets its port. Changes to the servers take effect after a restart of the radio. TCI Setting Console name Default Values What it does TCI server TCI ON ON, OFF Port below; restart to apply TCI port TCI_PORT 50001 1–65535 TCP port of the TCI server TCI swap I/Q TCI_IQINV OFF ON, OFF Mirror the spectrum sent to TCI clients TCI TX level TCI_TXLEVEL 100 1–200 % HPSDR Setting Console name Default Values What it does HPSDR server HPSDR ON ON, OFF UDP 1024; restart to apply HPSDR LNA to IF gain HPSDR_LNA OFF ON, OFF Let the HPSDR client's LNA gain set the radio's IF gain HPSDR swap I/Q HPSDR_IQINV OFF ON, OFF Mirror the spectrum sent to HPSDR clients Hamlib and console Setting Console name Default Values What it does Hamlib (rigctld) HAMLIB ON ON, OFF TCP 4532; restart to apply Console CONSOLE ON ON, OFF TCP 8081; restart to apply Web interface Setting Console name Default Values What it does Web UI UI ON ON, OFF Restart to apply Web UI port UI_PORT 8080 1–65535 Restart to apply Web UI HTTPS port UI_TLS_PORT 8443 0–65535 For a browser microphone on another device; 0 = off (restart to apply)"},{"p":"Connecting-other-programs.html","t":"Connecting other programs","h":"Logging programs","a":"logging-programs","x":"More → Logging programs sends what happens on the radio to other programs over UDP. Each setting is a list of host:port destinations separated by ; ; leave it empty for off. WSJT-X UDP speaks WSJT-X's own network protocol, so GridTracker, JTAlert and the WSJT-X links of Log4OM and N1MM+ work with the radio: a heartbeat, the status in FT8/FT4/FT2, every decode and every logged QSO in any mode. GridTracker on the Pi: 127.0.0.1:2237 ; on another computer: its address and port 2237; a multicast group such as 224.0.0.1:2237 reaches several programs. Clicking a CQ in GridTracker answers it, and its halt TX stops transmitting. ADIF over UDP sends each QSO you log as one ADIF record, for loggers with an ADIF UDP input (Log4OM, N1MM+, DXKeeper and others). Only new QSOs are sent; edits and imports are not. Setting Console name Default Values What it does WSJT-X UDP WSJTX_UDP (empty) text GridTracker, JTAlert, loggers: host:port, several separated by ; (e.g. 127.0.0.1:2237 or 224.0.0.1:2237). Empty = off ADIF over UDP ADIF_UDP (empty) text Loggers with an ADIF UDP input (Log4OM, N1MM+…): host:port; each QSO you log is sent. Empty = off"},{"p":"Console-commands.html","t":"Console commands","h":"Console commands","a":"","x":"Console commands The console is for set-up, status and calibration. Open it with telnet <radio> 8081 , or type into the terminal running jesdr in the foreground. Commands are not case-sensitive. Every setting can also be set by its console name (see All settings ): <NAME> <value> , for example WPM 20 ; <NAME> \"\" empties a text setting; set lists them all. status radio state, TX source, power/SWR, connected clients, audio underrun counts, which I2C bus is in use freq <hz> tune mode <USB|LSB|CW|CWR|AM|FM|DIGI|JS8|FT8|FT4|FT2|JTTY|RTTY|PSK|RADE> bw <hz> if <0-100> audio <0-100> drive <1-100> mic <0-50> agc <OFF|SLOW|MED|FAST> tx | rx key / un-key with the local mic tune on|off carrier at the tune power for the tune time twotone on|off two-tone test, 700 + 1900 Hz USB at the Drive (dummy load) mute on|off rit on|off|clear|<hz> receive incremental tuning xit on|off|clear|<hz> transmit incremental tuning (adds to split) band [<80m..10m> [1-3]] band stacking registers (no arguments: list them) maxvswr <1.0-10.0|0> SWR protection threshold (0 disables: not recommended) swrreset clear an SWR trip swrscan [points|stop] SWR across the band the dial is on ifauto learn auto IF gain: learn the band noise now smcal s9 | clear | <dB> S-meter on this band: calibrate to S9 / clear / set dB txcal start [W] [bands] PA power calibration into a DUMMY LOAD txcal stop|status bridgecal <W> while TUNE is keyed: match the power reading to a wattmeter (sets BRIDGE) lpfsweep start [W] [A,B,C,D] sweep the output low-pass filters into a DUMMY LOAD lpfsweep show [all] | stop | status the stored result (all: every frequency) diag pa|rx|bridge|audio|bias [...] diagnostics (diag help; see Diagnostics) diag freq [MHz] | freq apply frequency calibration from WWV/WWVH (see PA power and calibration) diag show <test> | stop | status diag bias [adjust|manual] | bias done [base_mA now_mA] PA bias adjustment txplan the transmit band plan in use cluster [status|hist|reconnect|send <text>|on|off] cwtext <text> | cwstop send text in CW / stop zerobeat CW/CWR: tune onto the signal the zero-beat indicator reads vk play N [repeat] | vk rec N | vk listen N | vk stop | vk delete N rec start|stop|toggle|list|delete <name> ft8 <verb> ... cq, hunt on|off, reply, tx, text, enable, halt, parity, txhz, dx, report, log, clear jtty <verb> ... send <text>, stop, txhz <hz>, clear js8 [status] the link to JS8Call (JS8 mode) js8 <verb> ... send <text>, speed slow|normal|fast|turbo, halt, launch, inbox, calls, raise, clear [band|msgs|calls|all] headset the headset / USB sound card: what is chosen, and the devices, numbered headset phones|mic <n|name|off> choose the headphones or microphone (n from that list) reporter [status] FreeDV Reporter: the connection and how many stations rtty <verb> ... send <text>, stop, txhz <hz>, clear psk <verb> ... send <text>, stop, txhz <hz>, clear cty <call> country-file lookup cty update|reload download the newest country file / read the file again gps setclock | gps grid set the clock / MYGRID from the GPS now solar [refresh|on|off] band conditions: SFI, A/K, MUF, HF day/night clock [check|set|rtcwrite|rtcread|sync] the Pi clock and RTC against network time system poweroff|reboot shut down or restart the Pi (un-keys and saves first) system stop|relaunch stop jeSDR (the Pi stays on) / restart jeSDR set list every setting save write the settings now exit stop the daemon help list the commands The web UI cannot send internal commands or exit ; it stops jeSDR only through More → Shut down ."},{"p":"Diagnostics.html","t":"Diagnostics","h":"Diagnostics","a":"","x":"Diagnostics More → Diagnostics has five self-tests for the radio's hardware (PA health, receiver health, SWR bridge check, the LPF sweep, audio and I²C). The PA bias adjustment and the frequency calibration , which use the same machinery, are on the Calibration page. One runs at a time. Each result is kept on the radio ( ~/jeSDR/data/diag_<test>.json ) and shown on the page the next time you open it. Rows are marked OK , Check or Fault , and the findings under each table say what to look at. Tests that transmit ask for a second tap and stop at once with Stop (or the Stop on the transmit strip). While a test runs nothing else can key, tune or change the mode; the SWR protection is held off during the transmitting tests, which stop by themselves on an SWR above 3:1. The PA health, receiver health and SWR bridge checks compare with the previous run, so run them once while the radio works well to have a baseline."},{"p":"Diagnostics.html","t":"Diagnostics","h":"PA health","a":"pa-health","x":"Needs a dummy load rated for the board's full power (25 or 40 W). About two minutes. With the radio receiving, then keyed with no drive, it measures the supply current. The difference is the PA's idle (bias) current . On each band (all but 60 m), it steps the drive 20, 30 … 100 % and measures the power, the supply current and voltage. For each band it reports the power at full drive, the current, the efficiency (output power over the extra DC power the PA draws), the supply voltage under load and the 1 dB compression point : the drive where the power falls 1 dB short of what the low-drive steps predict, i.e. where the PA stops getting louder. The compression point is measured from the PA's best gain between 20 and 60 % drive, so the lower gain at very low drive (the push-pull pair near its crossover) isn't mistaken for compression. It points out: almost no idle current (bias off, an open MOSFET or supply), an idle current that has moved since the last check (drifting bias, failing MOSFET), a PA that limits at 60 % drive or less on 80–17 m, efficiency under 20 %, a supply that drops more than 1 V under load, a band well below the others on 80–17 m, and any band that has lost a quarter of its power since the last check. 15–10 m give less power and saturate sooner by design (the push-pull PA's losses rise with frequency), so they are judged only against their own last check. Current, bias and efficiency need the INA260 power monitor; without one only the power is measured."},{"p":"Diagnostics.html","t":"Diagnostics","h":"Receiver health","a":"receiver-health","x":"Put a dummy load or a 50 Ω terminator on the antenna jack. It does not transmit; about 15 seconds. On each band it captures the receiver's IQ and measures the noise floor (dBFS/Hz, the median from 1 to 18 kHz either side of the dial) and any narrow spurs 15 dB or more above it, with their frequency. Each band is shown against the median of the others. More than 8 dB less noise means the band has lost gain (its low-pass filter relay, a solder joint, the front end); more than 8 dB more noise, or a spur 20 dB or more up, is interference made inside the radio. A band that has moved more than 4 dB since the last check is marked too. Some slope across 80–10 m is normal. The IF gain stays as you set it."},{"p":"Diagnostics.html","t":"Diagnostics","h":"SWR bridge check","a":"swr-bridge-check","x":"Dummy load : with a 50 Ω dummy load on the jack, a 5 W carrier on each band. The reflected reading should stay near zero; an SWR above 1.3 means the bridge is out of balance there (or the load isn't 50 Ω at that frequency), so the SWR meter and protection read high. Open jack : take the dummy load off and tap Open jack . A 2 W carrier for a moment on each band. The reflected reading should be close to the forward one; under 70 % means the reflected side under-reads, so the SWR meter and protection would read low into a bad antenna. The table shows both halves (the bridge's raw counts, 0–1023). The bridge's power calibration is BRIDGE ( PA power and calibration )."},{"p":"Diagnostics.html","t":"Diagnostics","h":"LPF sweep (low-pass filters)","a":"lpf-sweep-low-pass-filters","x":"The Low-pass filters section of More → Diagnostics (or search the settings for \"lpf\") measures the transmitter's four output low-pass filters and points out one that looks defective, damaged, built with a wrong or misplaced part, or not attenuating as it should. Filter Bands LPF_A 15, 12, 10 m LPF_B 20, 17 m LPF_C 40, 30 m LPF_D 80, 60 m You need a 50 Ω dummy load rated for 25 W or more on the antenna jack (no antenna, tuner or amplifier). Carrier : 1–10 W; 5 W is plenty. The power reading can briefly go above it (the drive is corrected at each frequency, and the PA's gain changes from band to band), hence the 25 W dummy load. Filters : B, C and D are selected. LPF_A is always swept: it is the reference the others are measured against. Tick the three checks and tap Start sweep , then Tap again to transmit . The radio keys a carrier and steps it from 1.5 to 36 MHz in 500 kHz steps, plus the centre of every band. Each filter is swept from just below its own bands upward (LPF_D from 3 MHz, C from 6, B from 12, A over the whole range): its passband and the harmonics it has to stop. At each frequency it holds each filter in circuit in turn and averages the SWR bridge's forward and reflected readings. The relays and the frequency only ever change with the carrier muted. A run takes three to five minutes (it times the radio's audio delay at the start and waits that long at each change), with a short rest in receive every 45 seconds. It transmits outside the amateur bands too, into the dummy load only. How it measures. The bridge sits after the filters, so its reading is the PA's output times the filter's response. The PA's output alone changes by several dB across HF (LPF_A's own curve shows it), so a filter can't be judged from its reading by itself. LPF_A passes everything up to about 30 MHz, so it serves as a reference: each other filter's reading divided by LPF_A's at the same frequency and drive is that filter's own response, with the PA and the bridge cancelled. In a filter's own bands expect it within a dB or so of LPF_A, either way: the filter is a slightly different load on the PA. The bridge cannot read much more than about 35 dB down at 5 W; deeper parts of the stopband are reported as \"at least\" that ( lpfsweep show all lists every frequency). For each filter it reports: Loss in its own bands against LPF_A (should be under 1.5 dB; over 2.5 dB is a fault). LPF_A can't be measured against itself: if it were lossy, the other filters would all read high in their bands, so the median of those readings is its figure. −3 dB , where it rolls off above the top of its range, from its own level in its bands. 2nd and 3rd harmonics of its lowest band, the hardest case: 12 dB or more at the 2nd and 20 dB or more at the 3rd is expected. Above 30 MHz LPF_A is rolling off too, so the figure there is a lower bound. LPF_A's own stopband is above the sweep's range: only its passband is checked. SWR in its own bands (a mismatched filter shows a higher SWR than LPF_A at the same frequency). Findings : dips inside the passband, a filter that passes almost nothing (relay not closing, an open connection), and what has changed since the previous sweep . Run it once on a radio that works well to have a baseline. If the radio's SWR bridge turns out to be ahead of the filters, the forward reading hardly changes with the filter; the sweep notices and judges each filter instead by how strongly it reflects above its cutoff (a high SWR there is right). The last result is kept on the radio ( ~/jeSDR/data/lpf_sweep.dat , and the one before it as lpf_sweep.dat.prev ) and shown when you open the page. A stopped sweep shows what it measured but is not saved. The SWR protection is held off during the sweep (a filter in its stopband may show a high SWR on purpose); the sweep keeps the power low instead. It stops on an SWR above 3.5 through LPF_A in its passband (check the dummy load), no output power, no bridge readings, or battery protection. While it runs not"},{"p":"Diagnostics.html","t":"Diagnostics","h":"Audio and I²C","a":"audio-and-i²c","x":"Does not transmit or tune; about 5 seconds. Codec inputs : receive, mic and the audio from programs such as WSJT-X (jeSDR Transmit): level (RMS), peak, DC offset and clipped samples. Clipping on receive means lower the IF gain or use ATT; a DC offset over 2 % points at a coupling capacitor or the codec's input. Audio drop-outs during the test and since start (speaker, capture, jeSDR Receive). I²C : the Si5351 (0x60), the SWR bridge (0x08) and the INA260 (0x40) are each probed 50 times, with each device's failed transactions since start. A device that misses probes has an intermittent fault: wiring, pull-ups, a noisy supply, or RF getting into the bus."},{"p":"Diagnostics.html","t":"Diagnostics","h":"Console","a":"console","x":"diag pa [bands] diag rx [bands] diag freq [MHz] | apply diag bridge load|open [W] diag audio diag show <test> diag stop | status | help Bands: e.g. 80m,40m,20m or all ."},{"p":"Digital-modes.html","t":"Digital modes","h":"Digital modes","a":"","x":"Digital modes FT8, FT4, FT2, JTTY, RTTY, PSK and FreeDV RADE V1 run on the radio itself: pick them from the mode sheet. Set My call sign and My grid first ( More → Station ). FT8 and FT4 need the radio's clock right to within about a second; Raspberry Pi OS keeps it with internet time, and in the field a GPS can. JS8 works with JS8Call running on the radio: see JS8 below. To use WSJT-X or fldigi instead, choose DIGI and see Connecting other programs ."},{"p":"Digital-modes.html","t":"Digital modes","h":"FT8, FT4 and FT2","a":"ft8-ft4-and-ft2","x":"Decodes appear at the end of each slot (15 s FT8, 7.5 s FT4, 3.75 s FT2): time, SNR, time offset, audio frequency, message and the sender's country. CQs are green, messages to you red, your own transmissions amber. In the sender's call, bold orange means never worked, dimmed means worked on this band and a dotted underline worked on another band. All / CQ + me filters the list. Hold a decode for the station's details. Long country names are shortened (\"Fed. Rep. of Germany\" shows as Germany); the full name is in the station's details. Stations with a long or unusual call (such as PI75VRZA, sent as a \"nonstandard\" call) can be answered like any other. Making a QSO CQ calls CQ in your next slot, or tap a decode to answer it. Replies, reports, RR73 and 73 then follow automatically, and the QSO is logged when you send RR73 or 73. Halt stops at once. A CQ with no answer stops after 20 tries, any other message after 6. The radio only transmits while TX on is lit. TX even/odd is your slot; − / + moves your audio frequency (the red line on the scope, or drag it). 1–6 pick the next message by hand; DX call… sets the station, Free text… sends up to 13 characters, Log QSO logs now. On a small screen such as the sBitx's 800×480 the controls are compacted so they fit without scrolling: the message buttons show only how each message ends ( 1 EN54 , 2 −10 , … 6 CQ ; the whole message is in the status line above them), the buttons read DX… , Text… and Log , and the list leaves out the time-offset column. Map shows a world map of the grids heard (yellow), grids in your log (green, red when logged today) and your own grid. It works without internet. Tuning: choosing FT8, FT4, FT2, JTTY, PSK or RADE tunes to that mode's usual frequency on the band ( Tune to mode frequency ). In the FT modes the tuning is locked ( Lock tuning in FT modes ): the knob, a drag and the arrow keys don't move the dial, and a tap on the waterfall moves the TX offset instead. Typed frequencies, the band buttons and CAT still tune; VFO lock on the Quick page turns the FT lock off for a while. FT2 is the open FT2 of WSJT-X Improved 3.1 (FT4's coding at twice the speed). It is experimental and needs a clock within about ±60 ms."},{"p":"Digital-modes.html","t":"Digital modes","h":"Automatic operating","a":"automatic-operating","x":"FT8 automation Behaviour ANS (default) answers and finishes QSOs as above OFF tapping a decode only fills in the station and the next message; you press TX on and pick the messages CQRESP adds a Hunt button: the radio answers CQs by itself, works and logs each QSO, then moves to the next CQ until you press Halt Hunting skips calls already worked ( Hunt skips : this BAND, ANY band or NONE), calls that did not answer you in the last 15 minutes and CQs aimed elsewhere; of the rest it answers the strongest, preferring the words in Hunt prefers (e.g. POTA,DX ) and calls never worked. CQ word adds a word to your CQ ( CQ POTA , CQ DX ); CQ every 2–4 calls only in every 2nd–4th slot. TX watchdog (10 minutes) stops automatic transmitting when you have not touched anything for that long: automatic operation still needs you at the station."},{"p":"Digital-modes.html","t":"Digital modes","h":"Portable activations","a":"portable-activations","x":"Set Activating (POTA, SOTA, WWFF or IOTA) and Your reference . Your CQ says CQ POTA , every other CQ sends the reference, and every QSO (FT8 or logged by hand) is logged with MY_SIG , MY_SIG_INFO and the matching MY_*_REF field. The logbook's Activations tab downloads each day's file for the program (see Logbook ). Transmit audio Setting Console name Default Values What it does FT8/FT4/FT2 TX audio FT8_TXF 1500 200–3000 Transmit offset above the dial, Hz JTTY TX audio JTTY_TXF 1500 200–3000 Transmit offset above the dial, Hz Tuning Setting Console name Default Values What it does Tune to mode frequency MODEQRG ON ON, OFF Choosing FT8, FT4, FT2, JS8, JTTY, PSK or RADE goes to that mode’s usual frequency on the band Lock tuning in FT modes FTXLOCK ON ON, OFF In FT8, FT4 and FT2 the knob, waterfall drag and scroll don’t move the dial, and a tap on the waterfall moves the TX offset. Typed frequencies, band buttons and CAT still tune Automatic operating Setting Console name Default Values What it does FT8 automation FT8_AUTO ANS OFF, ANS, CQRESP OFF: tapping a decode only fills in the station, you press TX on. ANS: answers and finishes QSOs. CQRESP: also Hunt — answers CQs by itself CQ word FT8_CQ (empty) text CQ POTA, CQ DX, CQ NA, CQ 123… (1–4 letters or 3 digits); empty = plain CQ CQ every FT8_CQEVERY 1 1–4 Call CQ in every Nth of your slots and listen in between (1 = every slot) Hunt skips FT8_SKIP BAND NONE, BAND, ANY Stations already worked: on this BAND, on ANY band, or NONE Hunt prefers FT8_PREFER (empty) text CQ words to answer first, e.g. POTA,DX Worked recently FT8_RECENT 24 0–720 Hours a contact counts as recent (dimmer in the decode list) TX watchdog FT8_WDOG 10 0–60 Automatic transmitting and hunting stop this many minutes after you last pressed something (0 = off). You must be at the station Portable activations Setting Console name Default Values What it does Activating XOTA OFF OFF, POTA, SOTA, WWFF, IOTA POTA, SOTA, WWFF or IOTA: CQ says so, every other CQ sends your reference, and QSOs are logged with it Your reference XOTA_REF (empty) text Park, summit or island, e.g. US-1234 (up to 10 characters go on air)"},{"p":"Digital-modes.html","t":"Digital modes","h":"RTTY","a":"rtty","x":"Baudot RTTY, 45.45 baud and 170 Hz shift by default, as audio FSK on upper sideband with mark the higher tone. One frequency (the dial plus RTTY center ) is used to receive and transmit. Tune: tap a signal on the scope and the M and S lines move to it; − / + nudge 5 Hz. The panel shows how far a copied signal is from the lines. Rev swaps mark and space. Receive: the text runs on in the panel. The bar is the signal quality; its tick is the squelch, below which text is hidden. Send: Type… or a macro keys the radio, sends the text and un-keys. More text sent meanwhile goes in the same over; Stop ends it."},{"p":"Digital-modes.html","t":"Digital modes","h":"PSK","a":"psk","x":"BPSK31, BPSK63 or BPSK125 on upper sideband at the dial plus PSK center . The panel works like RTTY's. Tap a signal to put the red line on it, drag the line, or nudge 1 Hz with − / +. AFC pulls in a signal up to about one baud away and follows drift. RTTY and PSK share the eight macros ( LABEL|text , with {CALL} , {RST} , {MYCALL} , {MYGRID} ; hold a button to edit it) and the Call and RST fields. RTTY Setting Console name Default Values What it does RTTY center RTTY_TXF 1500 200–2900 Between mark and space, Hz above the dial (receive and transmit); tap a signal on the scope to set it RTTY speed RTTY_BAUD 45.45 45.45, 50, 75, 100 Baud: 45.45 is the amateur standard RTTY shift RTTY_SHIFT 170 170, 200, 425, 850 Hz between mark and space: 170 is the amateur standard RTTY reverse RTTY_REV OFF ON, OFF Swap mark and space (for an upside-down signal) Unshift on space RTTY_USOS ON ON, OFF Back to letters after a space, receiving and sending RTTY squelch RTTY_SQL 30 0–100 Hide text below this signal quality (0 shows everything, noise too) PSK Setting Console name Default Values What it does PSK center PSK_TXF 1000 200–2900 Hz above the dial (receive and transmit); tap a signal on the scope to set it PSK speed PSK_MODE BPSK31 BPSK31, BPSK63, BPSK125 BPSK31 (the usual), BPSK63 or BPSK125 PSK squelch PSK_SQL 30 0–100 Hide text below this signal quality (0 shows everything, noise too) PSK AFC PSK_AFC ON ON, OFF Follow a signal up to about a baud off the line Macros Setting Console name Default Values What it does Macro 1 RTM1 CQ|CQ CQ CQ DE {MYCALL} {MYCALL} {MYCALL} K text LABEL|text, for RTTY and PSK; {CALL} {RST} {MYCALL} {MYGRID} Macro 2 RTM2 ANS|{CALL} {CALL} DE {MYCALL} {MYCALL} K text Same as macro 1 Macro 3 RTM3 RPT|{CALL} DE {MYCALL} UR {RST} {RST} {MYGRID} {MYGRID} {CALL} DE {MYCALL} K text Same as macro 1 Macro 4 RTM4 TU|{CALL} TU 73 DE {MYCALL} SK text Same as macro 1 Macro 5 RTM5 QRZ|QRZ? DE {MYCALL} K text Same as macro 1 Macro 6 RTM6 AGN|AGN AGN K text Same as macro 1 Macro 7 RTM7 TEST|CQ TEST {MYCALL} {MYCALL} text Same as macro 1 Macro 8 RTM8 EXCH|{CALL} {RST} {RST} {MYGRID} text Same as macro 1"},{"p":"Digital-modes.html","t":"Digital modes","h":"JTTY","a":"jtty","x":"Keyboard chat in a narrow (about 125 Hz) 4-GFSK signal with error correction, compatible with WSJT-X's JTTY. Each line is one station's message, growing as frames arrive. Tap a line to transmit on its frequency; Type a message… sends up to 80 characters at a time, CQ sends a CQ, Stop clears the queue. Choosing JTTY tunes to its calling frequency on the band ( Tune to mode frequency ). These are still preliminary: Band 80 m 40 m 30 m 20 m 17 m 15 m 12 m 10 m MHz 3.575 7.090 10.140 14.090 18.100 21.090 24.920 28.090 There is none on 60 m; there the dial stays where it is."},{"p":"Digital-modes.html","t":"Digital modes","h":"JS8","a":"js8","x":"JS8 is JS8Call's keyboard-to-keyboard mode: FT8-style weak-signal frames carrying free text, directed messages ( W1ABC SNR? , @ALLCALL CQ ), heartbeats, relays and a message inbox. The modem is JS8Call itself (JS8Call-improved, a separate GPL program), running on the radio's desktop: it controls the radio through jeSDR's Hamlib server, exchanges audio through jeSDR's PipeWire devices like WSJT-X, and jeSDR reads and drives it through JS8Call's TCP API . Nothing of JS8Call is built into jeSDR. Set-up (once) Install JS8Call: run the jeSDR installer again and answer yes to Install JS8Call (it puts JS8Call-improved's ARM64 AppImage in /opt/js8call and adds it to the menu). If JS8Call had no settings yet, the installer sets the rig and the API up for jeSDR. In JS8Call, Settings : Radio : rig Hamlib NET rigctl , network server 127.0.0.1:4532 , PTT method CAT , mode Data/Pkt (or None). Audio : input jeSDR Receive , output jeSDR Transmit , as for WSJT-X . Reporting → API : Enable TCP Server API and Accept TCP Requests , port 2442 (if you change it, set JS8Call API , JS8_API , to match). General : your call sign and grid. Using it Choose JS8 in the mode sheet. It tunes to the band's JS8 frequency ( Tune to mode frequency ) and connects to JS8Call; Open JS8Call starts JS8Call on the radio's desktop if it isn't running (log in to the desktop first). The panel's tabs: Band : what JS8Call hears, one line per offset, growing as frames arrive (the line ends at JS8Call's ♢ end mark). The number on the right is the SNR. Tap a line to answer its station. Messages : directed messages (to you in red, also @ALLCALL, @HB and groups) and what you send (amber). Messages to you also pop up. Calls : the stations JS8Call has heard, newest first. Inbox : JS8Call's message inbox. Clear empties the list on show (band lines, messages or calls; JS8Call's own inbox stays). Entries older than JS8 entries kept ( JS8_STALE , 30 minutes; 0 = keep them) go by themselves. Type a message… sends through JS8Call at its next cycle (JS8 is upper case; the chips add @ALLCALL , SNR? , GRID? , INFO? and so on). CQ sends @ALLCALL CQ CQ CQ <grid> , HB a heartbeat, Stop stops JS8Call transmitting. Slow / Normal / Fast / Turbo set JS8Call's speed (25 / 50 / 80 / 160 Hz wide; 30 / 15 / 10 / 6 s frames). Both stations need the same speed. Your transmit offset is the red JS8 line on the scope: tap the scope or drag the line to move it, or use − JS8 1500 Hz + . It is the same as JS8Call's offset both ways ( JS8 offset , JS8_TXF ). The band plan checks the actual JS8 signal (offset and width) before JS8Call can transmit. While in JS8, JS8Call asking for PKTUSB or USB keeps the radio in JS8. QSOs are logged by JS8Call (or its logging programs); jeSDR's own log form offers MFSK / JS8. Setting Console name Default Values What it does JS8 offset JS8_TXF 1500 100–2900 Where JS8Call transmits, Hz above the dial; kept the same as JS8Call’s offset JS8Call API JS8_API 127.0.0.1:2442 host:port JS8Call's TCP API. Empty = no link JS8 entries kept JS8_STALE 30 0–1440 minutes Band lines, messages and stations heard older than this are cleared (0 = keep) Band 80 m 40 m 30 m 20 m 17 m 15 m 12 m 10 m JS8 (MHz) 3.578 7.078 10.130 14.078 18.104 21.078 24.922 28.078 JS8Call's default 60 m frequency (5.363 MHz) is not a US channel, so on 60 m the dial stays where it is."},{"p":"Digital-modes.html","t":"Digital modes","h":"FreeDV RADE V1","a":"freedv-rade-v1","x":"RADE is FreeDV's machine-learning digital voice: about 1.5 kHz wide, with good speech down to low SNR. It is built into every jeSDR release. Choose RADE (it tunes to the band's FreeDV frequency). Tune within about ±50 Hz of the other station. Talk with the mic PTT. Set the level with Mic gain ; the equalizers, compressor and noise reduction do not apply to RADE. The panel shows sync, SNR and the frequency offset while receiving, and the speaker plays the decoded speech. While you transmit the speaker plays your own voice at TX monitor , never the modem signal. Sideband. FreeDV's convention is upper sideband on every band, and that is the default. Some nets below 10 MHz still use lower sideband, so the RADE panel has USB / LSB / Auto buttons ( RADE sideband , RADESB ). Auto uses LSB below 10 MHz and USB above, like voice SSB, and the button shows which one it is using. Both stations must use the same sideband, or neither decodes the other. The passband on the waterfall shows the side, and CAT programs read the mode as USB or LSB to match. The sideband doesn't change during a transmission. Setting Console name Default Values What it does RADE sideband RADESB USB USB, LSB, AUTO USB on every band (FreeDV’s convention), LSB, or Auto: LSB below 10 MHz and USB above"},{"p":"Digital-modes.html","t":"Digital modes","h":"FreeDV Reporter","a":"freedv-reporter","x":"FreeDV Reporter lists the FreeDV stations on the air. With FreeDV Reporter on ( FDVR ON , More → FT8 and digital): Reporter in the RADE panel opens the list: frequency, call sign, grid and distance, whether the station is transmitting (red), whom it last heard and at what SNR, and its message. This band shows the stations within 250 kHz of the dial, All every band. Tap a station to tune to it. The button shows a green dot and the number of stations while connected. The stations also show as green labels on the scope (red while they transmit), with the cluster spots; tap one to tune to it. You are listed while the radio is in RADE , with MYCALL , MYGRID , your frequency (on air, with a transverter's offset), when you transmit, and Reporter message ( FDVR_MSG ). In any other mode jeSDR hides you and only watches. Without MYCALL and MYGRID it only watches. A station asking you to QSY (from its FreeDV Reporter window) pops up. jeSDR does not yet send or decode the call sign RADE carries in its end-of-over, so stations do not report hearing you by call, and jeSDR does not report the stations it hears. reporter on the console shows the connection. Setting Console name Default Values What it does FreeDV Reporter FDVR OFF ON, OFF Show FreeDV stations; list yourself while in RADE Reporter message FDVR_MSG (empty) text Your message on FreeDV Reporter Reporter server FDVR_HOST qso.freedv.org host[:port] The server (WebSocket, port 80)"},{"p":"Digital-modes.html","t":"Digital modes","h":"Mode frequencies","a":"mode-frequencies","x":"Band FT8 FT4 FT2 PSK RADE 80 m 3.573 3.5755 3.578 3.580 3.625 60 m 5.357 – – – 5.4035 40 m 7.074 7.0475 7.052 7.070 7.177 30 m 10.136 10.140 10.144 10.142 – 20 m 14.074 14.080 14.084 14.070 14.236 17 m 18.100 18.104 18.108 18.100 18.118 15 m 21.074 21.140 21.144 21.070 21.313 12 m 24.915 24.919 24.923 24.920 24.933 10 m 28.074 28.180 28.184 28.120 28.330"},{"p":"Display-and-themes.html","t":"Display and themes","h":"Display and themes","a":"","x":"Display and themes More → Display holds the look of the screen. These settings are kept in each browser, so the radio's touchscreen and a phone can differ. Setting What it does Themes… chooses the colors (below) Waterfall contrast AUTO (default) follows the band's signal-to-noise, so plain noise stays dark; − / + set a fixed contrast (higher shows weak signals more strongly) Scope colors HEAT colors the area under the trace by strength, like the waterfall; PLAIN doesn't Waterfall speed 20 , 10 or 5 updates a second on this screen. Fewer means less work for the computer drawing it and less data over Wi-Fi. The radio's own screen starts at 10 (it keeps the Pi noticeably cooler), other devices at 20; each screen keeps its own choice. The battery saver still drops every screen to 5 Band privilege ribbons All classes, My class (from US license class ) or Off. With another country's band plan, one row shows where you may transmit DX cluster spots on the scope show or hide the spot labels in this browser On-screen keyboard AUTO shows it on touchscreens without a keyboard"},{"p":"Display-and-themes.html","t":"Display and themes","h":"Themes","a":"themes","x":"Themes… offers Midnight (the default), Outdoor (POTA), Night (red), High contrast, Ocean, Amber, Green phosphor, and Icom, Yaesu, Kenwood, Elecraft and Heathkit style. Each has its own waterfall colors. For night use pick Night (red) . Outdoor (POTA) is for operating in sunlight, on a park or summit activation: a white screen with black text, heavier lettering, thicker borders and a waterfall that runs from white through green to dark red, so signals still show on a screen in the sun. A radio that used the old Daylight theme gets it automatically. Customize opens the selected theme in an editor with a live preview: every color, the waterfall palette and a red filter for night use. Save it as your own theme. Export gives the theme as text, and Import… on another browser takes it in."},{"p":"Equalizer.html","t":"Equalizer","h":"Equalizer","a":"","x":"Equalizer Two five-band parametric equalizers: one for what you hear (receive) and one for your microphone (transmit). Open them from the Equalizer tile in the voice modes, or More → Equalizer → Open the equalizer . Each has a low shelf, three peaks and a high shelf, ±12 dB each, starting at 200 Hz, 500 Hz, 1 kHz, 1.8 kHz and 2.8 kHz. Turn the receive or transmit equalizer On . Drag a band up or down for its gain. Tap a band's frequency to pick it, then use − / + to move its frequency (50 Hz–5 kHz) and Q (0.3–8; narrow = high). For the shelves Q sets the slope. Flat zeroes the gains; Reset bands puts the frequencies and Q back. The receive EQ works in the voice modes (after the noise reduction); the transmit EQ shapes the microphone in the voice modes, after the compressor. Neither applies to RADE or the data modes. Receive Setting Console name Default Values What it does Receive EQ RXEQ OFF ON, OFF Equalizer on the speaker audio (voice modes) RX band 1 RXEQ1 0 -12–12 Low shelf, dB RX band 2 RXEQ2 0 -12–12 dB RX band 3 RXEQ3 0 -12–12 dB RX band 4 RXEQ4 0 -12–12 dB RX band 5 RXEQ5 0 -12–12 High shelf, dB Transmit Setting Console name Default Values What it does Transmit EQ TXEQ OFF ON, OFF Equalizer on the microphone TX band 1 TXEQ1 0 -12–12 Low shelf, dB TX band 2 TXEQ2 0 -12–12 dB TX band 3 TXEQ3 0 -12–12 dB TX band 4 TXEQ4 0 -12–12 dB TX band 5 TXEQ5 0 -12–12 High shelf, dB Each band's frequency and Q are kept as RXEQ1F … TXEQ5F (Hz) and RXEQ1Q … TXEQ5Q (Q × 10, so 10 is Q 1.0)."},{"p":"Headset-and-USB-audio.html","t":"Headset and USB audio","h":"Headset and USB audio","a":"","x":"Headset and USB audio A USB headset or USB sound card plugged into the Pi can be the radio's headphones, its microphone, or both. Choose them under More → Headset & USB audio . Setting Console name Default Values What it does Headphones PHONES OFF OFF or an output Plays what the speaker plays on that output Headphone level PHONESLVL 0 −20…20 dB Added to the volume knob's level Speaker with headphones PHONESSPK OFF ON, OFF OFF: the speaker jack goes quiet while the headphones play Microphone HSMIC OFF OFF or an input Takes the mic jack's place for transmitting Headset mic gain HSMICGAIN 0 −20…30 dB The headset mic's gain ( MIC is the mic jack's) Headphones. They get exactly what the speaker would: the receiver, the CW sidetone and the voice monitor ( TXMON ), at the speaker's level (the volume knob, or the fixed sidetone level while sending), plus Headphone level . The receiver's audio is blanked around each transmit/receive switch the same way, so there are no clicks. Microphone. In the voice modes (USB, LSB, AM, FM, RADE) the headset's microphone replaces the mic jack. Key the radio as usual: the mic's PTT, the PTT button, CAT or the console. In the digital modes the transmit audio still comes from the program playing into jeSDR Transmit . Choosing the device. The lists show every output and input in the Pi's PipeWire session, USB devices first (marked (USB) ). The settings remember the device, not a port, so: Unplugged, it shows Not connected and the speaker and mic jack take over. Plugged back in, it carries on by itself. The radio never sends its audio to another device instead, so the headphones' audio can never reach jeSDR Transmit . The Pi's desktop can still use the same headset; PipeWire mixes them. On the console , headset lists the devices with numbers and shows what is in use; headset phones 1 or headset mic 1 chooses one, and headset phones off turns the headphones off."},{"p":"Headset-and-USB-audio.html","t":"Headset and USB audio","h":"Choosing a headset","a":"choosing-a-headset","x":"Most USB headsets and USB sound cards work without drivers: look for USB Audio Class 1 or 2 (\"plug and play\", \"works with Chromebook / Linux\"). A wireless headset with its own USB dongle works the same way. Very power-hungry devices can upset the Pi; a powered USB hub helps. Models sBitx owners have reported working include the Logitech H390, H540 and H600, Jabra Evolve 20/30 II/40, Poly (Plantronics) Blackwire 3210/5220, EPOS Sennheiser SC 60 and SC 165 USB, Microsoft LifeChat LX-3000, the EKSA E1000 and the Sabrent USB stereo adapter (for a wired headset or microphone)."},{"p":"Headset-and-USB-audio.html","t":"Headset and USB audio","h":"If it doesn't work","a":"if-it-doesnt-work","x":"Nothing in the lists: jeSDR finds devices through the radio user's PipeWire session. status shows programs' audio: PipeWire: … ; if it says it is waiting, see Troubleshooting . Too quiet or too loud: use Headphone level and Headset mic gain . The device's own volume (on the headset, or in the desktop's sound settings) applies too. A slight delay compared to the speaker: PipeWire adds about 20–30 ms. For CW at high speed the speaker jack has the shortest sidetone delay."},{"p":"index.html","t":"jeSDR wiki","h":"jeSDR wiki","a":"","x":"jeSDR wiki jeSDR turns an sBitx HF transceiver into a network transceiver with its own touch-friendly screen. It runs on the radio's Raspberry Pi 4 or Pi 5, drives the hardware, and serves the radio to SDR, logging and digital-mode programs on your network: TCI, HPSDR Protocol 1 and Hamlib. FT8/FT4/FT2, JS8 (with JS8Call), JTTY, RTTY, PSK, a CW decoder with macros, FreeDV RADE V1, a voice keyer, DX cluster spots and a logbook are built in. Project website: www.jeSDR.com . This wiki explains every screen and setting and how to use them. The same pages are the operating manual built into the radio: open https://<radio>:8443/manual , or tap Manual on any settings page; it works without internet. A searchable copy is at jeSDR.com/manual . jeSDR is closed source; downloads are on the releases page ."},{"p":"index.html","t":"jeSDR wiki","h":"Getting started","a":"getting-started","x":"Install jeSDR from the SD card image, the one-line installer or the package. Open the radio's screen, or https://<radio>:8443/ from any browser on your network. Set your call sign and grid: More → Station ( Station and logbook ). Pick your country's transmit band plan: More → Band plan ( Band plan ). Before the first transmission into an antenna, run the PA power calibration into a dummy load."},{"p":"index.html","t":"jeSDR wiki","h":"Using the radio","a":"using-the-radio","x":"The main screen : header, scope and waterfall, control strip, band and mode, volume Quick page : your own shortcuts Receive : gain, auto IF gain, filters, noise reduction, squelch Transmit : drive, mic, compressor, PTT, tune, SWR protection, a browser microphone, CTCSS Band plan : country transmit limits and US privilege ribbons Equalizer : receive and transmit equalizers CW, voice keyer and recordings Digital modes : FT8, FT4, FT2, JS8, JTTY, RTTY, PSK, RADE Tuning, knobs and MIDI Display and themes"},{"p":"index.html","t":"jeSDR wiki","h":"Station and connections","a":"station-and-connections","x":"Station, logbook, country file and GPS Headset and USB audio : a USB headset or sound card as headphones and microphone Clock and band conditions : network time, the RTC, solar flux, K index and MUF Connecting other programs : WSJT-X, SDROxide, GridTracker, loggers, audio routing, network servers PA power and calibration Diagnostics Power, backup, shut down and About"},{"p":"index.html","t":"jeSDR wiki","h":"The radio and its SD card","a":"the-radio-and-its-sd-card","x":"Opening the case and swapping the SD card SD card images and backups : writing the image, starting from USB, copying or backing up the whole card Managing the jeSDR service : stop, start, restart, start at boot, status and logs from a terminal sBitx hardware and upgrades : specifications, microphones, Raspberry Pi 5, Touch Display 2 No RF output : settings, self-tests and the PA chain"},{"p":"index.html","t":"jeSDR wiki","h":"Reference","a":"reference","x":"All settings A–Z Console commands Safety and limitations Troubleshooting"},{"p":"index.html","t":"jeSDR wiki","h":"Help and community","a":"help-and-community","x":"Questions, ideas and 3D-printable parts (Pi 5 side plates, the Touch Display 2 bezel) are shared on the Discord server . Videos are on the Radio Electronics Hub YouTube channel . Report a bug from the project website ."},{"p":"index.html","t":"jeSDR wiki","h":"How settings work","a":"how-settings-work","x":"Every setting lives on the radio, so the touchscreen, a laptop and a phone all see the same values; a change in one browser shows in all of them at once. Open More (bottom right) for the settings menu. Each setting has a plain name, a line of help and a ☆ that adds it to the Quick page . Search at the top of the menu finds a setting by name, help text or common words (\"swr\", \"volume\", \"power\"). Each setting also has a console name (shown in the tables on these pages), which you can type on the console , e.g. WPM 20 . jeSDR is Copyright © 2026 J. Kujawa, W9JES. It is an independent project, built to run on sBitx hardware, and is not affiliated with or endorsed by the makers of the sBitx."},{"p":"Installing-and-updating.html","t":"Installing and updating","h":"Installing and updating","a":"","x":"Installing and updating jeSDR runs on the sBitx's Raspberry Pi 4 or Raspberry Pi 5 under Raspberry Pi OS 64-bit (Bookworm or later). On a Pi 5 the Pi needs a 5 V supply that can give 5 A. jeSDR drives the GPIO through the kernel's GPIO device, so no GPIO library (WiringPi) is needed; SPI must stay off ( dtparam=spi=on in config.txt takes the pins the filters and the volume knob use). The SD card image is made on a Pi 4: on a Pi 5, run the one-line installer once after the first start. Nothing else may use the audio codec or the GPIO at the same time, so remove any other radio program from the desktop autostart."},{"p":"Installing-and-updating.html","t":"Installing and updating","h":"Choose a way to install","a":"choose-a-way-to-install","x":"Use it when Keeps your data SD card image a fresh start on a new card no: it erases the card One-line installer you already run Raspberry Pi OS on the radio yes Package (.deb) the codec is already set up yes All three come from the releases page ."},{"p":"Installing-and-updating.html","t":"Installing and updating","h":"SD card image","a":"sd-card-image","x":"Download jesdr-<version>-sdcard.img.xz . Write it to a microSD card (16 GB or more) with Raspberry Pi Imager ( Use custom ) or balenaEtcher. Put the card in the radio and switch on. The first start takes a little longer: the card is expanded, and the radio makes its own HTTPS certificate and SSH keys. Open jeSDR from the desktop icon or the menu. To put the card in the radio see Opening the case ; to write a new card without opening it, or to copy or back up the whole card, see SD card images and backups . Writing an image erases the card. Your settings, log and voice messages are in ~/jeSDR/data : make a backup first, or update with the button instead (below), which keeps them."},{"p":"Installing-and-updating.html","t":"Installing and updating","h":"One-line installer","a":"one-line-installer","x":"On the radio, in a terminal: curl -fsSL https://drexjj.github.io/jeSDR/install.sh | sudo bash It checks the system, installs what jeSDR needs (including PipeWire and its development files, libpipewire-0.3-dev , so jeSDR can also be built on the Pi), sets up the WM8731 codec if it is not set up yet (other programs use jeSDR's own PipeWire audio devices; no loopback cards), downloads the latest package, checks its SHA-256 and installs it, then downloads the country file. It then offers to install JS8Call (JS8Call-improved's ARM64 AppImage, a separate GPL program, about 100 MB) for the JS8 mode ; run it again later to add or update JS8Call. If it changed the audio set-up it offers to reboot. Options go after bash -s -- , for example | sudo bash -s -- --yes : Option Does --yes answers yes to every question --deb FILE installs this package instead of downloading the latest --user NAME runs jeSDR as this user (default: the user who ran sudo) --no-hw leaves the codec and I²C set-up alone --no-js8call does not offer JS8Call"},{"p":"Installing-and-updating.html","t":"Installing and updating","h":"Package","a":"package","x":"sudo apt install ./jesdr_<version>_arm64.deb It installs to /opt/jeSDR and runs as the service jesdr under the first normal user (or the user named in /etc/jeSDR/user )."},{"p":"Installing-and-updating.html","t":"Installing and updating","h":"Opening the radio's screen","a":"opening-the-radios-screen","x":"On the radio: the jeSDR desktop shortcut or the menu entry (Internet / Ham Radio) opens the radio in its own Chromium window. From another device: open https://<radio>:8443/ . The first time, the browser warns about the certificate (the radio makes its own); choose Advanced → Proceed , once per device. http://<radio>:8080/ redirects there. HTTPS is what lets a phone or laptop use its own microphone ( Transmit ). Full screen on the radio (kiosk): add an autostart entry that runs /opt/jeSDR/jesdr-app --kiosk , for example in ~/.config/autostart/jesdr-kiosk.desktop : [Desktop Entry] Type=Application Name=jeSDR kiosk Exec=/opt/jeSDR/jesdr-app --kiosk Press Alt+F4 to leave kiosk mode. Every open browser shows the same radio."},{"p":"Installing-and-updating.html","t":"Installing and updating","h":"Remote desktop (VNC)","a":"remote-desktop-vnc","x":"To operate the radio, a browser on the other device is better than a remote desktop: the radio sends only the data and the other device draws it. For the Pi's desktop itself (other programs, settings), turn on VNC in the Raspberry Pi menu → Preferences → Raspberry Pi Configuration → Interfaces → VNC , then connect with a VNC viewer (for example RealVNC Viewer) to the radio's address or <hostname>.local and sign in with the Pi's user name and password. The moving waterfall keeps VNC busy, so close the viewer when you're done ( Troubleshooting )."},{"p":"Installing-and-updating.html","t":"Installing and updating","h":"Updates","a":"updates","x":"More → About → Software updates → Check now , then Install . The radio also looks once a day by itself ( Check for updates daily , UPDATECHK ) and says when there is a new version. Settings, log, calibration and voice messages are kept. Updates are signed: the radio installs only a package whose manifest carries the jeSDR release signature and whose SHA-256 matches. If a new version does not start within a minute, the previous one is put back. By hand: sudo apt install ./jesdr_<version>_arm64.deb ."},{"p":"Installing-and-updating.html","t":"Installing and updating","h":"Kernel I2C and the clock","a":"kernel-i2c-and-the-clock","x":"The radio's oscillator, SWR bridge, power monitor and real-time clock (RTC) share two GPIO pins. The Linux kernel drives that bus, with this line in /boot/firmware/config.txt (the sBitx image's own line): dtoverlay=i2c-rtc-gpio,ds1307,i2c_gpio_sda=13,i2c_gpio_scl=6 It makes the bus and binds the clock on it. Only one overlay may use those pins: with a second one (such as dtoverlay=i2c-gpio,... ) on GPIO 13 and 6 the RTC disappears. The installer asks before adding it, and an update adds it once on an sBitx that has no I2C line yet (keeping a backup, config.txt.jesdr-backup ). Earlier jeSDR versions added dtoverlay=i2c-gpio,bus=3,... , which runs the radio but has no clock: an update changes it to the line above, or removes it when an RTC line is there too (backup: config.txt.jesdr-rtc-backup ). It takes effect at the next restart. I2C does not need turning on in raspi-config (that is for the Pi's own I2C pins, GPIO 2 and 3): the package loads the i2c-dev module the radio's bus needs. status on the console shows the bus in use (for example i2c: /dev/i2c-2 ). At start-up jeSDR sets the Pi's clock from the RTC (unless network time is in already), so the clock is right in the field before GPS or the internet; at shutdown it writes the Pi's clock to the RTC if that session had network time. The clock tool shows the RTC and can set it by hand."},{"p":"Installing-and-updating.html","t":"Installing and updating","h":"Where your data lives","a":"where-your-data-lives","x":"File Contents ~/jeSDR/data/jesdr.ini settings ~/jeSDR/data/hw_settings.ini transmitter and S-meter calibration ~/jeSDR/data/jesdr_log.adi the logbook ~/jeSDR/data/voice/ voice keyer messages ~/jeSDR/data/recordings/ recordings ~/jeSDR/data/bigcty.dat the country file ~/jeSDR/data/jesdr_cert.pem , jesdr_key.pem the HTTPS certificate"},{"p":"Managing-the-jeSDR-service.html","t":"Managing the jeSDR service","h":"Managing the jeSDR service","a":"","x":"Managing the jeSDR service jeSDR runs on the Pi as a system service called jesdr . It starts by itself at every power-up and keeps running whether or not a screen or browser is open. These commands control it from a terminal on the radio or over SSH."},{"p":"Managing-the-jeSDR-service.html","t":"Managing the jeSDR service","h":"Commands","a":"commands","x":"What Command Stop jeSDR sudo systemctl stop jesdr Start jeSDR sudo systemctl start jesdr Restart jeSDR sudo systemctl restart jesdr Don't start at boot sudo systemctl disable jesdr Start at boot sudo systemctl enable jesdr Check its status sudo systemctl status jesdr Start at boot, and start now sudo systemctl enable --now jesdr Don't start at boot, and stop now sudo systemctl disable --now jesdr Watch its log live sudo journalctl -u jesdr -f Stop stops jeSDR immediately. The radio un-keys and saves its settings first. Start starts it again. Restart stops and starts it. Use it after changing a file by hand, or to recover from a problem. Disable keeps jeSDR from starting when the Pi boots. It doesn't stop a jeSDR that is running now. Enable makes jeSDR start when the Pi boots again (the installer enables it). Status shows whether jeSDR is running, since when, and its last few log lines, including any errors. Press q to leave it. Enable and start ( enable --now ) does 5 and 2 in one step. Disable and stop ( disable --now ) does 4 and 1 in one step. Live log shows jeSDR's messages as they happen, for troubleshooting. Press Ctrl+C to leave it. sudo journalctl -u jesdr -b shows everything since the Pi started."},{"p":"Managing-the-jeSDR-service.html","t":"Managing the jeSDR service","h":"Good to know","a":"good-to-know","x":"The jeSDR desktop shortcut starts the service if it isn't running, even when it is disabled. To keep jeSDR off, don't open the shortcut. After a crash the service restarts by itself within a few seconds. A stop is different: jeSDR stays stopped until you start it again. Settings → Shut down → Stop jeSDR does the same as systemctl stop from the radio's own screen or a browser (see Power, backup, shut down, About ). Installing or updating jeSDR restarts the service for you. Editing jeSDR's files by hand ( ~/jeSDR/data/hw_settings.ini , jesdr.ini ): jeSDR reads them only when it starts, and writes them itself (settings when they change; hw_settings.ini after a PA calibration or a reference crystal change), which can overwrite your edits. Stop jeSDR first, edit, then start it: sudo systemctl stop jesdr nano ~/jeSDR/data/hw_settings.ini sudo systemctl start jesdr Only one radio program at a time. jeSDR needs the audio codec and the GPIO for itself: stop it before running other software that drives the sBitx hardware directly, and start it again afterwards."},{"p":"No-RF-output.html","t":"No RF output","h":"No RF output","a":"","x":"No RF output The radio keys (the TX indicator lights, the relay clicks) but little or no power comes out. Work through the software checks first: most \"dead\" radios are a setting. Then the hardware checks below narrow it down to a stage of the PA chain. Always test into a 50 Ω dummy load with a wattmeter, never into an antenna, and keep transmissions short until you know the PA is healthy."},{"p":"No-RF-output.html","t":"No RF output","h":"Check the settings first","a":"check-the-settings-first","x":"SWR protection tripped? A trip holds the drive at 1 % until cleared ( Transmit → SWR protection ). Clear it, and find out why it tripped. Band plan. With a country plan set, the radio won't key outside it ( Band plan ). Drive and mode. Drive at 0, or a voice mode with no microphone audio (Mic gain at 0, the wrong mic path), gives no power. Tune sends a carrier regardless of the mic: start there. Battery protection holds the power down on a low supply ( Power & battery ). Calibration. A band whose PA power calibration is far too low gives almost no power on that band only. Restoring a backup from another radio brings its calibration with it."},{"p":"No-RF-output.html","t":"No RF output","h":"Let the radio test itself","a":"let-the-radio-test-itself","x":"More → Diagnostics ( Diagnostics ) measures the hardware for you, into a dummy load: PA health measures the PA's idle (bias) current and the power, current and efficiency on every band. Almost no idle current points at the bias or an open MOSFET; one weak band at a low-pass filter; all bands weak at the PA or its supply. LPF sweep finds a damaged or wrongly built output filter. SWR bridge check shows whether the power meter itself reads correctly. If you replace the MOSFETs, set the PA bias before anything else."},{"p":"No-RF-output.html","t":"No RF output","h":"Hardware checks (sBitx V3)","a":"hardware-checks-sbitx-v3","x":"These notes come from the sBitx 64-bit project wiki and may not be accurate for every board. Disconnect the power before every resistance or continuity test, and take care around the PA when it is powered. To get inside, see Opening the case . The full V3 PA schematic: v3-pa-circuit.png ."},{"p":"No-RF-output.html","t":"No RF output","h":"Look first","a":"look-first","x":"Burn marks on the IRF510s or transistors, cracked or bulging capacitors, discoloured resistors, damaged or loose transformer windings, and cold or broken solder joints. Replace anything that looks wrong before measuring."},{"p":"No-RF-output.html","t":"No RF output","h":"The IRF510 MOSFETs (Q16, Q17)","a":"the-irf510-mosfets-q16-q17","x":"A shorted IRF510 gets hot and draws a lot of current even at the lowest drive (PA health shows a high idle current). A MOSFET damaged by heat may simply not amplify: no extra current, no heat. With the power disconnected, on each IRF510: Continuity (diode or continuity mode) between gate–drain, gate–source and drain–source: there should be none. A beep or a low reading means a short. Drain to source (resistance mode, nothing on the gate): high resistance, ideally open. Diode test red lead on the source, black on the gate: note the reading; reversed, a good MOSFET reads high or OL ."},{"p":"No-RF-output.html","t":"No RF output","h":"The driver transistors","a":"the-driver-transistors","x":"With the radio transmitting a carrier, the base–emitter voltage of Q11 (BFR106) and Q12–Q15 (2N2219A) should be about 0.6–0.7 V . Much lower or higher points at that transistor or the resistors and capacitors around it."},{"p":"No-RF-output.html","t":"No RF output","h":"The T/R switch (PA supply)","a":"the-tr-switch-pa-supply","x":"When transmitting, the PA gets +12 V through Q9 (IRF4905), switched by Q10 and Q101 (2N7002): Q9: about 12 V on its drain (the metal tab) while transmitting. Always on suggests a short; always off, look at Q10. Q10: turns on while transmitting and pulls Q9's gate to about 0 V (V GS ≈ −12 V), which turns Q9 on. Q101: its gate should be high (~12 V) while receiving and low (~0 V) while transmitting; its source is always at ground. A drain that stays high when it shouldn't suggests a faulty Q101."},{"p":"No-RF-output.html","t":"No RF output","h":"Supply rails","a":"supply-rails","x":"+12 V: measure at the drains of Q16 and Q17. Too low: check the supply and look for a short. HV rail: measure it and check D20–D25 , C201 and the parts around them for a fault or an unusual voltage drop."},{"p":"No-RF-output.html","t":"No RF output","h":"With an oscilloscope","a":"with-an-oscilloscope","x":"Follow the signal from the input to the antenna jack: Q11 (BFR106) input: the RF should be there. If not, the fault is before the PA. Q12–Q15 (2N2219A pre-drivers): the signal should be larger here. Missing: a transistor or its bias. PA bias: at R75, R77 and D6 (5.6 V Zener), about 5.6 V on D6's banded side while transmitting. Gate drive on Q16 and Q17: weak or missing, check the driver and transformer T5 . Output transformer T7: no output, check its windings for continuity. Low-pass filters (L31–L41, C301–C308) and the antenna network (L402, L403): signal before but not after points at a filter part or a joint (the LPF sweep finds most of these). After replacing a part, check the idle current and set the bias before going back to full power."},{"p":"Opening-the-case.html","t":"Opening the case and swapping the SD card","h":"Opening the case and swapping the SD card","a":"","x":"Opening the case and swapping the SD card The Raspberry Pi and its microSD card sit inside the sBitx. You only need to open the case to swap the card, adjust the PA bias or work on the boards. To put a new image on the card without opening the case, see SD card images and backups . Before you start: Download a backup of your settings, log and calibration. Shut the radio down ( More → Shut down ) and unplug the power and the antenna. Work on a flat, static-free surface, and keep the screws in a tray. You need a Phillips screwdriver."},{"p":"Opening-the-case.html","t":"Opening the case and swapping the SD card","h":"1. Top screws","a":"1-top-screws","x":"On the top of the case, remove the two screws circled in red and loosen (don't remove) the two circled in blue."},{"p":"Opening-the-case.html","t":"Opening the case and swapping the SD card","h":"2. Bottom screws","a":"2-bottom-screws","x":"Turn the radio over. Remove the two screws circled in red and loosen the two circled in blue."},{"p":"Opening-the-case.html","t":"Opening the case and swapping the SD card","h":"3. Separate the halves","a":"3-separate-the-halves","x":"Gently pull the top and bottom sections apart. Don't force them: the display and speaker cables still join the two halves."},{"p":"Opening-the-case.html","t":"Opening the case and swapping the SD card","h":"4. Rest the top section on its speaker","a":"4-rest-the-top-section-on-its-speaker","x":"Lay the top section back on its speaker so the cables aren't strained. Support the screen so it can't tip over."},{"p":"Opening-the-case.html","t":"Opening the case and swapping the SD card","h":"5. Remove the SD card","a":"5-remove-the-sd-card","x":"The microSD slot is on the edge of the Raspberry Pi, next to the ribbon cable (circled). Pull the card straight out (tweezers or a small screwdriver help), and slide the new one in the same way round as the old one."},{"p":"Opening-the-case.html","t":"Opening the case and swapping the SD card","h":"Putting it back together","a":"putting-it-back-together","x":"Reverse the steps: seat the halves without pinching a cable, then fit and tighten the four red screws and tighten the four blue ones. Connect the power and switch on. A freshly written card takes a little longer on its first start (see Installing and updating )."},{"p":"PA-power-and-calibration.html","t":"PA power and calibration","h":"PA power and calibration","a":"","x":"PA power and calibration More → Calibration has six sections: PA power , the PA bias (PA_BIAS1), the Power meter (SWR bridge), the Frequency (reference crystal), the S-meter and the Receiver (birdie shift). The LPF sweep is on the Diagnostics page."},{"p":"PA-power-and-calibration.html","t":"PA power and calibration","h":"PA power calibration","a":"pa-power-calibration","x":"The PA's gain changes from band to band, so each band has its own power scale. The PA power section of More → Calibration sets them all so that Drive 100 % gives the same power on every band. Run it once on a new radio, after changing the PA or the low-pass filters, and whenever the power on some band looks wrong. Searching the settings for \"pa power\" finds it too. You need a dummy load rated for the full power, and a supply that can deliver about 6 A at 13.8 V. An inline wattmeter is useful for checking the result but is not needed: the radio measures with its own SWR bridge. sBitx board : DE or V2 (40 W) or V3 (25 W). Check the label on your board. This sets the target and the power limit. Target power : the board's rating, or less if you want. Bands : all except 60 m are selected; add 60 m if you use it (it calibrates on 5357 kHz). Tick the three checks: the dummy load is on the antenna jack, the supply can deliver the current, the radio is cool. Tap Start calibration , then Tap again to transmit . The radio sends a steady carrier on one band at a time. At each step it waits for the power to settle, then averages the forward power over short windows until two in a row agree, and moves the band's scale until the power is within 2.5 % of the target. Each band is on the air for 5–10 seconds, with a few seconds of receive between bands; a whole run takes one to two minutes. The page shows the power, SWR, supply voltage and step live, and each band's old and new scale when it finishes. A band whose PA cannot reach the target (often 10–15 m at 40 W) is set to the lowest scale that gives its maximum, and shows PA maximum . The run stops on an SWR above 3:1, no measurable power, no readings from the SWR bridge, or battery protection lowering the drive. Stop un-keys at once. The band in progress keeps its old scale; bands already done keep their new one. While it runs nothing else can key, tune or change the mode. The result is saved with each band's power limit, which the ALC holds the output under. It is part of the backup . Console: txcal start [W] [bands] (e.g. txcal start 25 40m,20m ), txcal stop , txcal status . Setting Console name Default Values What it does sBitx board PA_MODEL V3 DE, V2, V3 Sets the PA calibration’s power: DE and V2 40 W, V3 25 W. Check the label on your board"},{"p":"PA-power-and-calibration.html","t":"PA power and calibration","h":"PA bias adjustment","a":"pa-bias-adjustment","x":"More → Calibration → PA bias (PA_BIAS1) guides setting PA_BIAS1 on the sBitx V3 (two IRF510s), which is needed after replacing the MOSFETs. It follows the V3 bias guide, with the radio doing the keying and the measuring. You need: a 50 Ω dummy load (and a power meter, if you have one); a 12–13.8 V supply current-limited to 2 A; the case open and PA_BIAS1 found, with the heatsink in place. Turn PA_BIAS1 fully counter-clockwise (minimum bias), tick the checks and tap Start , then Tap again to transmit . The radio keys on 7.035 MHz with no drive at all (the calibration carrier at zero), so the mic and drive settings don't matter and only the bias current flows. After 1.5 s it measures the supply current for 2 s: the baseline . Turn PA_BIAS1 slowly clockwise. The page shows the increase over the baseline live on a 0–350 mA bar with the 150–250 mA target marked: under 150 mA it says turn clockwise ; between 150 and 250 mA it says in range ; above 250 mA it says turn counter-clockwise now . Let it settle a few seconds and tap Done — check it . The radio receives for 5 s, keys again and measures for 3 s. The result shows the baseline, the reading when you finished, and the reading after keying again. The radio flags a setting outside 150–250 mA, and a change of more than 30 mA between the end of the adjustment and the check (let it settle: a bias that keeps rising with temperature risks thermal runaway). Check that the heatsink is warm, not hot, then run PA health or the PA power calibration. Adjust again keeps the stored baseline ( ~/jeSDR/data/diag_bias.base ) for a touch-up without turning the pot back to minimum. PA health also reports the idle current against this baseline. It un-keys by itself if: the bias goes past 400 mA; it has been keyed for 5 minutes; any RF output appears (more than 1 W forward). Without an INA260 , put a meter in series with the supply. The radio still keys with no drive (with the same 5-minute limit). Type the two readings in mA (the keyed current with the pot at minimum, and the current now); the page works out the increase and guides you the same way, and Done records them. The 150–250 mA target is for the V3. For other boards, use their own bias figures. Console: diag bias , diag bias adjust , diag bias manual , diag bias done [base_mA now_mA] , diag show bias ."},{"p":"PA-power-and-calibration.html","t":"PA power and calibration","h":"Power meter (SWR bridge) calibration","a":"power-meter-swr-bridge-calibration","x":"sBitx boards' SWR bridges differ, so the power the radio shows can read high or low. More → Calibration → Power meter corrects it against an external wattmeter: Put a wattmeter and a dummy load on the antenna jack. Tap Tune for a steady carrier. Type what the wattmeter reads and tap Match . The radio sets SWR bridge ( BRIDGE , 25–400, default 100) so its reading matches. The forward reading is divided by it, so a higher value reads lower; you can also set it by hand. Then run the PA power calibration again, since it measures with this meter. SWR readings don't change (they are a ratio). Console: bridgecal <W> while TUNE is keyed, or BRIDGE <n> . Setting Console name Default Values What it does SWR bridge BRIDGE 100 25–400 Power meter calibration for your board: higher reads lower"},{"p":"PA-power-and-calibration.html","t":"PA power and calibration","h":"Frequency calibration","a":"frequency-calibration","x":"More → Calibration → Frequency (reference crystal) (or search the settings for \"frequency\"). Needs the antenna; it does not transmit. It tunes to the time stations WWV/WWVH: all of 2.5, 5, 10, 15, 20 and 25 MHz, or just 5, 10, 15 or 20 MHz. It measures each carrier's offset to a fraction of a hertz from 2.7 seconds of IQ, at two dial settings (which also confirms the direction). The offset divided by the frequency is the reference crystal's error in ppm; the median of the stations heard is used. WWV is usually best on 10–20 MHz by day and 2.5–5 MHz at night. Apply (console diag freq apply ) sets the Si5351's crystal frequency to match and saves it in hw_settings.ini ( [tcxo] cal ). It is accepted for 30 minutes after a measurement. The radio's own tuning steps add about 0.1 ppm of uncertainty, and ionospheric Doppler a little more: run it twice if the stations disagree. GPS gives the time, not a frequency, so it can't calibrate the crystal by itself. Console: diag freq [MHz] , diag freq apply , diag show freq ."},{"p":"PA-power-and-calibration.html","t":"PA power and calibration","h":"S-meter calibration","a":"s-meter-calibration","x":"Each band has its own S-meter calibration. The S-meter takes the IF gain out of its reading, so it reads the same at any IF gain; the Hamlib and TCI S-meters use the same reading. Feed a −73 dBm carrier (S9) from a signal generator into the antenna jack and tune it into the filter passband. Tap Calibrate this band to S9 . That band's calibration is set; Clear zeroes it. Repeat on each band you use, or set a band's dB by hand with its − and +. There is no longer one setting for all bands. A radio that had one ( SMCAL ) has it added to each band's calibration the first time the updated jeSDR starts, so the meter reads the same as before. Console: smcal s9 , smcal clear , smcal <dB> (this band), bare smcal shows this band's value."},{"p":"PA-power-and-calibration.html","t":"PA power and calibration","h":"Birdie shift","a":"birdie-shift","x":"Moves the radio's internal oscillator by a few Hz so its own spurious tones fall outside the audio you hear. Change it only if a steady tone sits on your signals. Setting Console name Default Values What it does 80 m SMCAL80M 0 -200–200 S-meter calibration on 80 m, dB 60 m SMCAL60M 0 -200–200 S-meter calibration on 60 m, dB 40 m SMCAL40M 0 -200–200 S-meter calibration on 40 m, dB 30 m SMCAL30M 0 -200–200 S-meter calibration on 30 m, dB 20 m SMCAL20M 0 -200–200 S-meter calibration on 20 m, dB 17 m SMCAL17M 0 -200–200 S-meter calibration on 17 m, dB 15 m SMCAL15M 0 -200–200 S-meter calibration on 15 m, dB 12 m SMCAL12M 0 -200–200 S-meter calibration on 12 m, dB 10 m SMCAL10M 0 -200–200 S-meter calibration on 10 m, dB Birdie shift BFO 0 -3000–3000 Moves the radio’s internal oscillator so its own spurious tones (birdies) fall outside the audio you hear. Change it only if a steady tone sits on your signals; Hz"},{"p":"Power-backup-shutdown-and-about.html","t":"Power & battery, backup, shut down and About","h":"Power & battery, backup, shut down and About","a":"","x":"Power & battery, backup, shut down and About"},{"p":"Power-backup-shutdown-and-about.html","t":"Power & battery, backup, shut down and About","h":"Power & battery","a":"power--battery","x":"For the field: protection from a stuck transmitter and a flat battery, a saver mode, and the Pi's own health. A badge next to the S-meter shows the most important of them; tap it to open this page. The top of the page shows the supply (with an INA260 power monitor fitted), the battery stage, the transmit power limit, the saver, the Pi's temperature and its power and speed flags, now and since start-up. Transmit timeout (10 minutes; 0 = off): after that long transmitting without a break (a stuck PTT, VOX or CAT program) the radio un-keys, stops the keyer, voice keyer and digital modes, and refuses to transmit while something keeps asking. The badge counts down the last minute. Battery protection (needs the INA260): choose LIFEPO4 or AGM . It watches the resting voltage (readings while transmitting, and just after, are left out): Stage LiFePO4 AGM What happens Getting low 12.8 V 12.0 V a warning Low 12.5 V 11.7 V drive held to 70 % (about half power); battery saver on (AUTO) Flat 12.0 V 11.4 V transmitting off, settings saved Shut down when flat : after 60 s on a flat battery the Pi shuts down cleanly, before the supply takes the SD card with it. Battery saver (OFF, ON, AUTO = when the battery is low): 5 waterfall updates a second instead of 20, and the screen's backlight at 40 % where the Pi can dim it (the official touch screens and other DSI panels). The badge shows ECO . Setting Console name Default Values What it does Transmit timeout TOT 10 0–30 Minutes of transmitting without a break before the radio un-keys itself (a stuck PTT, VOX or CAT program); 0 = off Battery protection BATT OFF OFF, LIFEPO4, AGM Battery type, for the INA260 supply voltage: warn, then half power and the saver, then no transmitting when it is flat. LIFEPO4: 12.8 / 12.5 / 12.0 V. AGM (sealed lead-acid): 12.0 / 11.7 / 11.4 V Shut down when flat BATT_SHUT OFF ON, OFF After 60 s on a flat battery, shut the Pi down cleanly before the supply takes the SD card with it Battery saver SAVER AUTO OFF, ON, AUTO Fewer waterfall updates (5 a second) and a dimmer screen. AUTO: when the battery is low"},{"p":"Power-backup-shutdown-and-about.html","t":"Power & battery, backup, shut down and About","h":"Backup","a":"backup","x":"Download a backup gives one file ( jesdr-backup-CALL-YYYYMMDD-HHMM.tar.gz ) with the settings (macros and band stacks included), the logbook, the transmitter and S-meter calibration and the voice keyer's messages. Keep a copy off the radio: SD cards fail. Recordings and the web certificate are not included. On the radio's own screen, this and the other download buttons (recordings, Export ADIF, activation files) open a Save dialog, so you can pick the folder or a USB stick. From another computer the file goes to that browser's downloads as usual. Restore from a file… checks the archive, keeps the files it replaces in ~/jeSDR/data/before-restore-* , puts the backup's in and restarts the radio. Restore on the same radio: the calibration belongs to its hardware."},{"p":"Power-backup-shutdown-and-about.html","t":"Power & battery, backup, shut down and About","h":"Shut down","a":"shut-down","x":"Restart the radio or Shut down the Pi. The radio un-keys and saves its settings first, and every open browser shows what is happening. Shut down before switching the power off: pulling the plug on a running Pi can damage its SD card."},{"p":"Power-backup-shutdown-and-about.html","t":"Power & battery, backup, shut down and About","h":"Stopping jeSDR only","a":"stopping-jesdr-only","x":"Under jeSDR only (the Pi stays on) : Restart jeSDR restarts just the jeSDR program. The radio is off the air for a few seconds and every open page reconnects by itself. Use it after changing a server setting that says \"restart to apply\". Stop jeSDR stops the program and leaves the Pi running, for example to work in a terminal, run another radio program that needs the audio codec, or copy files. The radio un-keys and saves its settings first; open pages show that jeSDR is stopped and reconnect by themselves when it is back. To start jeSDR again: open jeSDR from the desktop shortcut or the menu on the radio (it starts the service first), or run sudo systemctl start jesdr in a terminal or over SSH, or restart the Pi. jeSDR also starts by itself at every power-up. All the service commands are in Managing the jeSDR service . Console: system stop , system relaunch ."},{"p":"Power-backup-shutdown-and-about.html","t":"Power & battery, backup, shut down and About","h":"About","a":"about","x":"The version, whether FreeDV RADE is built in, connected clients, the SWR protection, supply and log size; a link to the project's website, www.jeSDR.com , for news, downloads and help (on the radio's own screen it asks before opening a window); Software updates (see Installing and updating ); and, at the bottom, the credits and the licenses of the software jeSDR includes. Setting Console name Default Values What it does Check for updates daily UPDATECHK ON ON, OFF Looks for a newer jeSDR once a day and says so here"},{"p":"Quick-page.html","t":"Quick page","h":"Quick page","a":"","x":"Quick page More → Quick is the first page of the settings menu: buttons and settings you reach for often, in the order you choose."},{"p":"Quick-page.html","t":"Quick page","h":"The buttons","a":"the-buttons","x":"Button What it does Tune sends a carrier at Tune power for Tune time seconds; while it is on, the button and a strip at the top of the menu show the power and SWR (see Transmit ) VFO A/B swaps between the two VFOs RIT turns receive incremental tuning on and off XIT turns transmit incremental tuning on and off ATT each tap steps the attenuator: off, 6, 12, 18 dB PRE each tap steps the preamp: off, 6, 12 dB Split receive on the VFO in use and transmit on the other one (on VFO A, transmit on B; on VFO B, transmit on A). Set the TX frequency by switching to the other VFO and tuning, then switch back. The line under the frequency shows split · TX and where you will transmit; PTT, the CW keyer, TUNE and the two-tone test all use it, and the privilege warning checks the TX frequency VFO lock locks the tuning; in FT8/FT4/FT2 it turns the automatic FT lock off and on Mute mutes the speaker ● Record starts and stops a recording (see Recordings ) Logbook opens the logbook TX scope a floating panel with the transmit audio waveform while you transmit, with the clip level (SSB), 100 % modulation (AM) or full deviation (FM) marked Not on the page at first, but available under Edit → Add to Quick : Two-tone test (see Transmit )."},{"p":"Quick-page.html","t":"Quick page","h":"RIT","a":"rit","x":"RIT turns it on and off; − / + on the RIT offset row move the receiver in 10 Hz steps (hold to run) and Clear sets 0. Only the receiver moves: the passband on the scope shifts, while the dial marker and your transmit frequency stay put. The header shows RIT +120 Hz while it is on. Console: rit on|off|clear|<hz> ."},{"p":"Quick-page.html","t":"Quick page","h":"XIT","a":"xit","x":"XIT is the opposite: only the transmitter moves. XIT turns it on and off; the XIT offset row works like RIT's (10 Hz steps, hold to run, Clear ). You keep listening on the dial while you transmit the offset away, for example to call a DX station \"up 1\" without split. It adds to split: in split the transmitter goes out on the other VFO plus the XIT offset. Every way of transmitting uses it (PTT, the CW keyer, TUNE, the two-tone test, the digital modes), and the privilege warning checks where you will actually transmit. The header shows XIT +1500 Hz · TX 14.251.50 while it is on. Console: xit on|off|clear|<hz> ; Hamlib z / Z . RIT, XIT and their offset rows are on the Quick page by default. If you have customized the Quick page, add them with Edit ; searching the settings for \"rit\" or \"xit\" also finds the offset rows."},{"p":"Quick-page.html","t":"Quick page","h":"Making it yours","a":"making-it-yours","x":"Tap the ☆ next to any setting anywhere in the menu to add it to Quick (★ means it is already there; tap again to remove it). Edit on the Quick page lists what is on it: ↑ ↓ reorder, ✕ removes. Add to Quick underneath searches every button and setting. Done saves. The list is kept on the radio ( QUICK ), so the touchscreen and every other screen share it."},{"p":"Receive.html","t":"Receive","h":"Receive","a":"","x":"Receive More → Receive holds the receiver's gain, filter, noise and squelch settings. Most of them are also tiles on the control strip."},{"p":"Receive.html","t":"Receive","h":"Gain","a":"gain","x":"Volume is the speaker; the left knob and the header's volume button set the same value. IF gain is the receiver's input gain (the codec ahead of the ADC). Set it so the band noise moves the S-meter a little; too much and strong signals overload, too little and weak ones fall into the ADC's own noise. The S-meter allows for the IF gain, so it reads the same at any setting. Attenuator (6, 12, 18 dB) helps when a very strong signal overloads the receiver: the waterfall goes solid and audio distorts. The sBitx has no RF attenuator, so this lowers the gain ahead of the ADC. Preamp (6, 12 dB) adds gain when the band noise barely moves the S-meter (often 10–15 m), as far as the IF gain leaves room. Attenuator and Preamp turn each other off."},{"p":"Receive.html","t":"Receive","h":"Auto IF gain","a":"auto-if-gain","x":"Auto IF gain keeps the band noise (the S-meter between signals) at the level it had when you turned it on. It moves the IF gain in the codec's 1.5 dB steps every few seconds: less gain on a noisy band, more on a quiet one, and a quick drop when the input nears clipping. It allows for narrow filters and pauses while you transmit. Set the IF gain where you like it on a typical band. Turn Auto IF gain ON. It learns that noise level. Learn now takes the level again from the band as it is now (console ifauto learn ). The row under it shows the noise in S-units, the target and the codec peak. Setting the IF gain by hand turns Auto IF gain off; the IF tile shows auto while it is on."},{"p":"Receive.html","t":"Receive","h":"Filter","a":"filter","x":"The receive filter is set by its width ( BW ), low edge ( LOWCUT ) and high edge ( HIGHCUT ), in audio Hz. Each group of modes keeps its own filter: SSB, CW, AM, FM, DIGI, FT8/FT4, JTTY, RTTY, PSK and RADE. SSB and data modes: Filter width moves the high edge above the low cut. CW, RTTY and PSK: the filter is centered on the signal (the CW pitch or the RTTY/PSK center) and follows it; width widens or narrows both sides. AM and FM: symmetric about the carrier; High cut sets each side."},{"p":"Receive.html","t":"Receive","h":"Noise","a":"noise","x":"Noise reduction 0–10. With type SPEC (spectral, for voice) about 8 dB less noise at 2, 16 dB at 6, 23 dB at 10; 4–6 suits most voice, and above about 8 the background starts to warble. LMS (adaptive line enhancer) suits weak CW, where a spectral NR tends to chop the tone. Noise blanker 0–10, for impulse noise (ignition, power-line pops). High levels can distort very strong signals. Auto notch removes steady tones (carriers, heterodynes) in the voice modes, several at once, even under speech. NR and the auto notch add about 22 ms of delay. Data modes and the audio for other programs (jeSDR Receive) always get the unprocessed audio."},{"p":"Receive.html","t":"Receive","h":"Squelch and tone","a":"squelch-and-tone","x":"Squelch for AM and FM, 0 = open. CTCSS receive : on FM, pass audio only while this tone is heard. Pick one of the 38 standard tones from the list."},{"p":"Receive.html","t":"Receive","h":"All receive settings","a":"all-receive-settings","x":"Gain Setting Console name Default Values What it does Volume AUDIO 20 0–100 Speaker volume (the left knob) AGC AGC SLOW OFF, SLOW, MED, FAST Automatic gain control speed IF gain IF 60 0–100 Receiver input gain Auto IF gain IFAUTO OFF ON, OFF Moves the IF gain to keep the band noise steady on the S-meter: less gain when the band is noisy or a signal would overload, more when it is quiet. Set the IF gain you like, then turn it on: it holds that noise level. Setting the IF gain by hand turns it off Attenuator ATT OFF OFF, 6, 12, 18 dB less receive gain: for very strong signals that overload the receiver (the waterfall turns solid, distortion). The S-meter allows for it Preamp PRE OFF OFF, 6, 12 dB more receive gain: when the band noise barely moves the S-meter (often 10–15 m). As far as the IF gain leaves room. The S-meter allows for it Filter Setting Console name Default Values What it does Filter width BW per mode 50–5000 Receive bandwidth, Hz (around the signal in CW, RTTY and PSK); each mode keeps its own Low cut LOWCUT per mode 0–4900 The filter’s low edge, Hz; the high edge stays put High cut HIGHCUT per mode 50–5000 The filter’s high edge, Hz; the low edge stays put Noise Setting Console name Default Values What it does Noise reduction NR 0 0–10 0 = off; higher removes more noise (Spectral: 4–6 for most voice; above 8 the background may warble) Noise reduction type NRMODE SPEC SPEC, LMS SPEC: spectral, best for voice. LMS: adaptive line enhancer, best for weak CW Noise blanker NB 0 0–10 0 = off; for ignition and power-line pops Auto notch ANF OFF ON, OFF Removes steady tones (voice modes) Squelch and tone Setting Console name Default Values What it does Squelch SQL 0 0–20 AM/FM squelch level, 0 = open CTCSS receive CTCSS_RX OFF OFF or one of the 38 standard tones (67.0–250.3 Hz) Tone squelch on FM: audio only when this tone is heard The S-meter calibration is on the Calibration page."},{"p":"SD-card-images-and-backups.html","t":"SD card images and backups","h":"SD card images and backups","a":"","x":"SD card images and backups The sBitx is a computer as well as a transceiver: its Raspberry Pi runs Raspberry Pi OS from a microSD card. An image is a complete copy of such a card (the operating system, jeSDR and its set-up) in one file. Writing an image to a card is like reformatting a computer's disk and restoring it: everything on the card is replaced. Use an image for a fresh start, a new or failed card, or to get back to a known-good system. To add jeSDR to a card that already runs Raspberry Pi OS, or to update it, you don't need an image: see Installing and updating . Your radio's What keeps it settings, log, calibration, voice messages More → Backup → Download a backup (one small file) everything on the card a whole-card copy (below) The calibration ( hw_settings.ini ) belongs to your radio's own boards, so keep a backup of it whatever else you do."},{"p":"SD-card-images-and-backups.html","t":"SD card images and backups","h":"Writing the jeSDR image","a":"writing-the-jesdr-image","x":"You need a computer (Windows, macOS or Linux), a microSD card of 16 GB or more (32 GB gives room for recordings and other programs) and a card reader. Download the SD card image from the releases page . Install Raspberry Pi Imager or balenaEtcher . Both write the compressed file ( .img.xz or .img.gz ) directly; there is no need to unpack it first. Write it: Raspberry Pi Imager: Choose OS → Use custom , pick the image; Choose storage , pick the card; Next . When it offers to customise the settings, choose No : the image is already set up. balenaEtcher: Flash from file , Select target , Flash . Eject the card, put it in the radio and switch on. The first start takes a little longer: the card is expanded to its full size, and the radio makes its own HTTPS certificate and SSH keys. Then restore your backup in More → Backup → Restore from a file… . The image is made on a Pi 4: on a Pi 5, run the one-line installer once after the first start. If the card isn't recognised, try another reader or USB port, and make sure the card is big enough. A card that fails to write twice is probably worn out: SD cards do fail, especially cheap ones."},{"p":"SD-card-images-and-backups.html","t":"SD card images and backups","h":"Without opening the case","a":"without-opening-the-case","x":"The Pi can start from a USB drive instead of its internal card. That lets you try an image without touching the card inside, or copy a new image onto the internal card from the radio itself."},{"p":"SD-card-images-and-backups.html","t":"SD card images and backups","h":"Start the radio from a USB drive","a":"start-the-radio-from-a-usb-drive","x":"Write the image to a USB drive of 16 GB or more, as above (choose the USB drive as the target). On the radio, open a terminal and run sudo raspi-config . Choose Advanced Options → Boot Order → USB Boot ( NVMe/USB Boot on newer versions), then Finish . If that gives an error, connect the radio to the internet, choose Update in raspi-config, then Advanced Options → Bootloader Version → Latest , reboot and try again. Shut down, plug in the USB drive and switch on. The first start from USB takes a few minutes. To go back to the internal card, remove the USB drive and restart. With no USB drive the Pi looks for one for a few seconds, then starts from the card."},{"p":"SD-card-images-and-backups.html","t":"SD card images and backups","h":"Copy the USB drive onto the internal card","a":"copy-the-usb-drive-onto-the-internal-card","x":"With the radio running from the USB drive: Back up anything you want from the internal card first (it is about to be replaced). Open the Raspberry Pi menu → Accessories → SD Card Copier . Copy From Device: the USB drive you started from (usually /dev/sda ). Copy To Device: the internal card ( /dev/mmcblk0 ). Tick New Partition UUIDs , check both choices again, and press Start . It asks before it overwrites the card. When it finishes, shut down, remove the USB drive and switch on: the radio starts from the freshly copied card."},{"p":"SD-card-images-and-backups.html","t":"SD card images and backups","h":"Backing up the whole card","a":"backing-up-the-whole-card","x":"A copy of the whole card brings back everything at once, including other programs and their settings. On the radio , to a USB drive at least as big as the card (it erases the drive): Raspberry Pi menu → Accessories → SD Card Copier , Copy From the internal card ( /dev/mmcblk0 ), Copy To the USB drive (usually /dev/sda ), Start . To restore, start from that drive and copy it back (above), or swap the card . On a computer , as an image file: take the card out of the radio and read it with Win32 Disk Imager ( Read ) on Windows, or dd on Linux or macOS: sudo dd if=/dev/sdX of=radio-backup.img bs=4M status=progress ( /dev/sdX is the card reader; check it with lsblk first, because dd to the wrong device erases it.) The file is the card's full size; write it back with Raspberry Pi Imager or balenaEtcher like any other image. A copy of your card contains your Wi-Fi passwords, the radio's own keys and certificate, and your log. Keep it for yourself; don't share it as an image for others."},{"p":"SD-card-images-and-backups.html","t":"SD card images and backups","h":"Moving to a new card","a":"moving-to-a-new-card","x":"More → Backup → Download a backup on the old card. Write the jeSDR image to the new card and start the radio with it. More → Backup → Restore from a file… with the backup. Programs you added yourself (WSJT-X, fldigi, JS8Call…) are not in the backup: install them again, or use a whole-card copy instead. Their own logs are in your home folder: ~/.local/share/WSJT-X/ , ~/.local/share/JS8Call/ and ~/.fldigi/ ."},{"p":"Safety-and-limitations.html","t":"Safety and limitations","h":"Safety and limitations","a":"","x":"Safety and limitations"},{"p":"Safety-and-limitations.html","t":"Safety and limitations","h":"Safety","a":"safety","x":"SWR protection covers every transmit path. A trip holds the drive at 1 % and ignores remote drive changes until it is cleared ( Transmit ). Transmit band plan : with a country plan set, the radio will not key outside it ( Band plan ). An HPSDR client that stops sending during transmit is un-keyed after 1 s; a TCI client that disconnects while keyed is un-keyed at once. A browser that keyed the radio is un-keyed if it disconnects or goes silent for 3 s. Stopping jeSDR (More → Shut down → Stop jeSDR, or the service) un-keys and saves first; a crash drops the T/R relay and the external PTT before the process exits. The speaker is held silent for the first 1.5 s after start-up. The PA power calibration transmits only after the three checks and a second tap, stops on an SWR above 3:1, and holds every other way of keying or tuning off while it runs. The transmit timeout un-keys every source after 10 minutes without a break; battery protection holds the power down on a low battery and stops transmitting on a flat one ( Power & battery ). Always make the first transmission on each interface into a dummy load with a wattmeter: send a single tone, check it lands on the correct side of the carrier and that the power follows the drive."},{"p":"Safety-and-limitations.html","t":"Safety and limitations","h":"Limitations","a":"limitations","x":"The built-in digital modes are FT8, FT4, FT2, JTTY, RTTY, PSK and RADE V1. JS8 is not built in: run JS8Call on the Pi in DIGI mode. For anything else use WSJT-X or fldigi through Hamlib, or a TCI/HPSDR program. RADE's end-of-over callsign is not sent or shown. TCI carries IQ, not receive audio; programs on the Pi use the jeSDR Receive / jeSDR Transmit audio devices. An HPSDR client is the master of the frequency: a retune from Hamlib or the console is not reported back to it (TCI clients are told). HPSDR clients see one VFO; split and RIT are available over TCI, split also over Hamlib. Only one program should transmit at a time. Browser audio is 16 kHz mono with 80–300 ms of delay: fine for voice and digital-mode monitoring, too slow for CW sidetone (none is sent)."},{"p":"Station-logbook-and-country-file.html","t":"Station, logbook, country file and GPS","h":"Station, logbook, country file and GPS","a":"","x":"Station, logbook, country file and GPS"},{"p":"Station-logbook-and-country-file.html","t":"Station, logbook, country file and GPS","h":"Station","a":"station","x":"More → Station : your call sign and grid, a transverter, the DX cluster and the logbook. My call sign and My grid are used by FT8 and the other digital modes, the macros ( {MYCALL} , {MYGRID} ), the log ( STATION_CALLSIGN , MY_GRIDSQUARE ), distances and bearings, and the DX cluster login. Set them first. Transverter : with a 2 m or 70 cm transverter on the 10 m output, set 2M (+116 MHz) or 70CM (+404 MHz), or CUSTOM with Transverter offset in kHz. While the dial is on 10 m (28–32 MHz) the header, band button, scope, ribbons and log show the on-air frequency, and the keypad takes it too (type 144.174 and the radio tunes 28.174). 10 m reaches 32 MHz for this."},{"p":"Station-logbook-and-country-file.html","t":"Station, logbook, country file and GPS","h":"DX cluster","a":"dx-cluster","x":"Turn Cluster connection ON (it needs My call sign ). The radio logs in to a telnet cluster node, ve7cc.net:23 by default; the node buttons fill in others, or type any DXSpider, AR-Cluster or CC Cluster node as host:port . Spots appear as labels under the band ribbons at their frequency: tap one to tune there, hold it for the station's details. They fade and drop after Spot age minutes. Console: cluster status|reconnect|send <text> (for example a node filter command). Setting Console name Default Values What it does My call sign MYCALL (empty) text Logged as STATION_CALLSIGN My grid MYGRID (empty) text Logged as MY_GRIDSQUARE Transverter XVTR OFF OFF, 2M, 70CM, CUSTOM On 10 m (28–32 MHz) show and log the frequency on 2 m (+116 MHz), 70 cm (+404 MHz) or a custom offset Transverter offset XVTR_OFS 116000 0–9000000 For CUSTOM: kHz added to the 10 m dial (e.g. 116000 for 144 MHz on 28 MHz) DX cluster Setting Console name Default Values What it does Cluster connection CLUSTER OFF ON, OFF The radio connects to a DX cluster node for spots Cluster node address CLUSTER_HOST ve7cc.net:23 text host:port; the node buttons above fill it in, or type another node Spot age CLUSTER_AGE 30 5–120 Minutes a spot stays on the scope"},{"p":"Station-logbook-and-country-file.html","t":"Station, logbook, country file and GPS","h":"Logbook","a":"logbook","x":"An ADIF log kept on the radio ( ~/jeSDR/data/jesdr_log.adi ), so every screen sees the same log. Open it with Log on the control strip. New QSO: the time (UTC, taken when you start typing the call), frequency, band and mode come from the radio; mode chips pick SSB, CW, AM, FM, FT8, FT4, FT2, RTTY or PSK31. RST defaults suit the mode and the power is the peak of your last transmission. While you type a call it shows whether you have worked it before and fills in name, QTH and grid from the last QSO. Under the call: its country, continent, zones, and distance and bearing from your grid. QRZ opens its qrz.com page in a new tab (no QRZ login is kept on the radio). Logbook: newest first; search by call; tap a QSO to edit or delete it. Export ADIF downloads the log; Import ADIF merges a file from another program, skipping QSOs already logged. Activations lists every park, summit or WWFF area per UTC day and downloads the file each program takes: POTA and WWFF an ADIF file ( CALL@REF-YYYYMMDD.adi ), SOTA its CSV. A POTA day under 10 QSOs is marked. New QSOs can also go to other logging programs as they are made; see Connecting other programs ."},{"p":"Station-logbook-and-country-file.html","t":"Station, logbook, country file and GPS","h":"Country file","a":"country-file","x":"Callsign lookups (country, zones and continent under decodes and spots, the log's DXCC fields, FT8's \"new one\" colors) use Jim Reisert AD1C's Big CTY file. It is a free download, not part of jeSDR; the installer fetches it, and a new one comes out a few times a year. The Country file section at the bottom of More → Station shows the version in use (search the settings for \"country\" to jump there). Download the latest : the radio fetches the newest Big CTY from www.country-files.com. Upload a file… : for a radio without internet, download bigcty-YYYYMMDD.zip from www.country-files.com/big-cty on another device and upload it here (the zip, or the cty.dat inside it). From a shell: sh /opt/jeSDR/tools/fetch_cty.sh ; console cty update ."},{"p":"Station-logbook-and-country-file.html","t":"Station, logbook, country file and GPS","h":"GPS","a":"gps","x":"FT8 and the other digital modes need the clock within about a second. At home the Pi gets the time from the internet; in the field a USB GPS receiver (a u-blox 7/8 or VK-162 \"puck\") gives it the time and your grid. Plug it in: gpsd finds it and jeSDR reads it. More → GPS shows a sky plot of the satellites (filled when used in the fix), their signal strengths, the fix, position, grid, altitude, the GPS time and how far the Pi's clock is from it. A GPS badge in the header is green with a 3D fix, amber with 2D and gray without; tap it to open the page. Use this grid sets My grid once; Set the clock now sets the clock at once. Set the clock from GPS (AUTO): when there is no internet time and the clock is 0.2 s or more off, it is set from the GPS. Grid from GPS (AUTO) keeps My grid set to the 6-character grid you are in, for portable and mobile use. GPS delay : how late the receiver's time message arrives. If FT8 decodes show DT around +0.1 or −0.1 s, adjust by that much. A receiver without a clear view of the sky may take several minutes for its first fix. To check the clock against network time, or to set the real-time clock (RTC), see Clock and band conditions . Setting Console name Default Values What it does GPS receiver GPS ON ON, OFF Read a USB GPS through gpsd Set the clock from GPS GPS_TIME AUTO AUTO, OFF AUTO: when there is no internet time and the clock is 0.2 s or more off Grid from GPS GPS_GRID OFF AUTO, OFF AUTO: keep MYGRID at your 6-character grid (portable and mobile) GPS delay GPS_DELAY 100 0–1000 ms a GPS report arrives after the second it describes; raise it if FT8 decodes show DT below 0 after a GPS clock set gpsd address GPS_HOST 127.0.0.1:2947 text host:port of gpsd (normally on the radio itself)"},{"p":"The-main-screen.html","t":"The main screen","h":"The main screen","a":"","x":"The main screen The same screen works on the radio's 800×480 touchscreen, a desktop browser and a phone; the layout adapts to the size."},{"p":"The-main-screen.html","t":"The main screen","h":"Header","a":"header","x":"From left to right: Item What it does Band ( 40m ) opens the band sheet: nine bands with three band-stacking registers each Mode ( LSB ) opens the mode sheet: USB, LSB, CW, CWR, AM, FM, DIGI, FT8, FT4, FT2, JTTY, RTTY, PSK, RADE Frequency tap it for a keypad; scroll the mouse wheel over a digit to change that digit. The line under it shows the VFO, the tuning step, RIT and locks jeSDR logo tap it to open jeSDR.com in a new window (on the radio's own screen it asks first); it shrinks, and hides on a phone, when the header is short of room Clock UTC and local time Band conditions solar flux, then K index and MUF; the dot is your band now (green Good, amber Fair, red Poor). Tap it for the details ( Clock and band conditions ) Supply voltage and current, with an INA260 power monitor fitted (amber below 11.5 V or above 15 V) Badges GPS, ● REC, battery stage, ECO, transmit timeout, Pi temperature; tap one to open its page S-meter S-units and dBm while receiving; power and SWR while transmitting, with the ALC meter under it Volume the speaker volume; tap it for a slider RX / TX press and hold to transmit (push-to-talk) On another device a 🔈 button next to PTT plays the receiver in that browser (see Connecting other programs )."},{"p":"The-main-screen.html","t":"The main screen","h":"Volume","a":"volume","x":"Tap the volume button for a tall slider; drag it, or tap anywhere outside to close it. The left knob on the radio moves the same setting ( AUDIO ). Double-tap the button to point the right knob at the volume."},{"p":"The-main-screen.html","t":"The main screen","h":"Band sheet","a":"band-sheet","x":"A band button goes to the register you used last on that band. Tapping the band you are already on steps to its next register, and the three registers of the current band are shown underneath to pick one directly. Each register remembers frequency, mode, filter, drive, tune power and IF gain; where you were is saved when you leave it. The registers live on the radio, so every screen shares them. SWR scan… is described on the Transmit page."},{"p":"The-main-screen.html","t":"The main screen","h":"Mode sheet and keypad","a":"mode-sheet-and-keypad","x":"Type kHz ( 14074 ) or MHz with a dot ( 14.074 ) and press Enter ."},{"p":"The-main-screen.html","t":"The main screen","h":"Scope and waterfall","a":"scope-and-waterfall","x":"The scope shows 24 kHz around the dial; the waterfall below it shows the recent past. Tap to tune there. Drag or scroll to tune in whole Tuning steps (the dial stays on the step grid: with a 1 kHz step it moves 1 kHz at a time). Pinch or − / + to zoom (24, 12, 6 or 3 kHz). Zooming in also sharpens the display. The shaded area is the receive passband; the dashed line is the dial. The colored ribbons along the top are the US band privileges (see Band plan ). In FT8, FT4, FT2, JTTY and PSK a red line marks the transmit offset; in RTTY two lines mark the mark (M) and space (S) tones. Drag a line, or its label, to move it; the dial stays put. With the DX cluster on, spotted calls appear as labels at their frequency; tap one to tune to it, hold it for the station's details. With AUTO contrast (the default) the waterfall's color range follows the band's noise and signals, so plain noise stays dark. Display settings are on the Display and themes page."},{"p":"The-main-screen.html","t":"The main screen","h":"Control strip","a":"control-strip","x":"The tiles along the bottom change with the mode: SSB, CW, the digital modes, RADE and AM/FM each have their own set. Drag or scroll a tile to change its value. Tap a tile to point the radio's right knob at it; push the knob (or wait 10 s) to go back to tuning. Some tiles open a sheet instead (Filter, Equalizer, Voice keyer). Log opens the logbook ; More opens the settings."},{"p":"The-main-screen.html","t":"The main screen","h":"Filter","a":"filter","x":"Tap Filter for width ( BW ), low cut and high cut; dragging the tile changes the width. Each group of modes keeps its own filter and gets it back when you return. In CW, RTTY and PSK the filter sits on the signal and follows it. See Receive ."},{"p":"The-main-screen.html","t":"The main screen","h":"Mode panels","a":"mode-panels","x":"In CW, FT8, FT4, FT2, JTTY, RTTY, PSK and RADE a panel opens above the control strip with that mode's decoder and buttons; ▾ makes it smaller. See CW and Digital modes ."},{"p":"The-main-screen.html","t":"The main screen","h":"Smaller screens","a":"smaller-screens","x":"The radio's 800×480 touchscreen: A phone puts the header on three rows:"},{"p":"Transmit.html","t":"Transmit","h":"Transmit","a":"","x":"Transmit More → Transmit : power and microphone, PTT, the tune carrier, SWR protection and the SWR scan, a microphone in another device's browser, and the FM repeater tone."},{"p":"Transmit.html","t":"Transmit","h":"Power and audio","a":"power-and-audio","x":"Drive (1–100 %) is the transmit power. After the PA power calibration , 100 % gives the same power on every band. Mic gain (0–50) is the codec's mic input level in percent, as on the earlier sBitx software. (A USB headset's microphone has its own gain: see Headset and USB audio .) Talk at your normal level and watch the TX scope on the Quick page : the peaks should just reach the clip line. Compressor (0–10) raises the average level of speech; 0 is off. CESSB (controlled-envelope SSB) holds the SSB peaks down so the average power can be higher for the same peak power. TX monitor (0–10) plays your own voice in the speaker while you transmit, at a modest level so it doesn't feed back into the microphone. Headphones are best; with the speaker, keep it low. The transmit equalizer shapes the microphone after the compressor."},{"p":"Transmit.html","t":"Transmit","h":"ALC meter","a":"alc-meter","x":"While transmitting, a thin bar under the power meter (and ALC on the transmit strip at the top of an open menu) shows how hard the transmitter is being driven: SSB and AM : the peak of your modulation against the voice clipper, from −20 dB to +10 dB. The amber zone starts at the clipper (two-thirds of the way along). Green means you are below it, with no limiting. Amber means the peaks are being clipped by that many dB: a few dB is speech processing. Red means more than 6 dB, which sounds distorted, so lower the Mic gain or the Compressor . FM is measured against full deviation. Every mode : when the power would pass the band's power limit, the ALC holds it down, and that reduction is added in. CW, digital modes and TUNE have no clipper, so the bar moves only when the power ALC acts. The figure ( ALC 3 dB ) is the total limiting, shown on the transmit strip and as the bar's tooltip. Hamlib programs can read it as the ALC level (0–1 for 0–10 dB)."},{"p":"Transmit.html","t":"Transmit","h":"PTT","a":"ptt","x":"External PTT keys GPIO 26 while transmitting, for an external amplifier. Mic PTT : whether the microphone's PTT keys the radio. The mic jack's wiring, and other microphones that fit it, are on sBitx hardware . The RX/TX button in the header is press-and-hold PTT. The transmit timeout ( TOT , 10 minutes) un-keys a stuck PTT; see Power and battery ."},{"p":"Transmit.html","t":"Transmit","h":"Tune","a":"tune","x":"Tune (on the Quick page , or console tune on ) sends a steady carrier at Tune power for Tune time seconds, for an antenna tuner. While it is on, the Tune button shows the power and SWR, and a red strip across the top of the menu shows them too, with a bar for the power and a Stop button. The menu covers the meter, so you can watch the tuner without closing it. The strip appears whenever you transmit with a menu open (the two-tone test, the SWR scan, PTT); the SWR turns amber at 2 and red at 3 or on a trip."},{"p":"Transmit.html","t":"Transmit","h":"SWR protection","a":"swr-protection","x":"On by default. If the SWR goes above Trip above SWR (1.5–10; 3.0 suits most antennas) on any transmit path, the drive drops to 1 % and remote drive changes are ignored. Status shows OK or the trip; fix the antenna, then Clear trip and set the drive back. Turning protection off takes two taps and is not recommended. Console: maxvswr , swrreset ."},{"p":"Transmit.html","t":"Transmit","h":"SWR scan","a":"swr-scan","x":"Scan SWR across this band (also in the band sheet) keys a carrier at SWR scan drive (10 %) and steps it across the band the dial is on in SWR scan points steps (41 points, about 9 s), drawing SWR against frequency and marking the lowest point. Tap the graph to tune there. A key, PTT, tuning or Stop ends it, and the dial, mode and drive are put back. The drive can be anything from 1 to 100 %. The normal SWR protection is held off while the scan runs, because a trip would end it at the first bad point, often a band edge. Instead the scan protects the PA itself: above 25 % drive, wherever the SWR goes past the trip level it drops to 25 % at once and goes back up when the match is good again. Every point is still measured."},{"p":"Transmit.html","t":"Transmit","h":"Two-tone test","a":"two-tone-test","x":"Two-tone test (on this page, or add it to the Quick page ) sends two equal tones, 700 Hz and 1900 Hz, on upper sideband at the normal Drive . It is the standard signal for checking an SSB transmitter's linearity: on a spectrum analyzer the intermodulation products show beside the two tones, and on an oscilloscope the envelope should be clean, un-flattened arches. Connect a dummy load rated for the power (and the analyzer through a suitable attenuator or sampler). Set the Drive you want to test. Tap Two-tone test . The header shows 2-TONE . It stops after Two-tone time seconds (30), or tap the button again, or press PTT. The peak envelope power (PEP) is twice the average, so a wattmeter that reads average power shows about half the PEP. The band plan, SWR protection and the power limits apply as for any transmission. Console: twotone on|off ."},{"p":"Transmit.html","t":"Transmit","h":"Talking from another device","a":"talking-from-another-device","x":"A phone, tablet or laptop can transmit with its own microphone. Browsers only allow the microphone over HTTPS, so open https://<radio>:8443/ and accept the certificate warning once. More → Transmit → Microphone for this browser : choose This device's mic and allow the microphone when the browser asks. Test mic shows the level. Browser's auto gain lets the device level your voice; Browser mic level ( WEBTXLVL ) is the gain at the radio. Hold PTT and talk (USB, LSB, AM, FM, DIGI or RADE). The audio goes through the usual chain: compressor, equalizer, CESSB. Releasing PTT, closing the page, or 1.5 s without audio (lost Wi-Fi) un-keys the radio. Each browser keeps its own choice; the radio's own screen stays on the radio's microphone."},{"p":"Transmit.html","t":"Transmit","h":"FM","a":"fm","x":"CTCSS transmit sends one of the 38 standard tones with FM, for repeaters that need one. Pick it from the list; CTCSS receive is on the Receive page."},{"p":"Transmit.html","t":"Transmit","h":"All transmit settings","a":"all-transmit-settings","x":"Power and audio Setting Console name Default Values What it does Drive DRIVE 40 1–100 Transmit power, % Mic gain MIC 30 0–50 Mic input level, % (as on the sBitx) Compressor COMP 0 0–10 Speech compression, 0 = off CESSB CESSB OFF ON, OFF Controlled-envelope SSB (more talk power) TX monitor TXMON 0 0–10 Hear your own voice while transmitting, 0 = off PTT Setting Console name Default Values What it does External PTT ePTT OFF ON, OFF Key an external amplifier Mic PTT LOCALPTT ON ON, OFF The mic PTT keys the radio Tune Setting Console name Default Values What it does Tune power TNPWR 20 1–100 Drive for TUNE, % Tune time TNDUR 5 2–30 TUNE stops by itself after this many seconds SWR protection SWR protection on/off, the trip level and Clear trip are on this page; console maxvswr . SWR scan Setting Console name Default Values What it does SWR scan drive SCANPWR 10 1–100 Drive while scanning, %; above 25 % it drops to 25 % wherever the SWR is past the trip level SWR scan points SCANPTS 41 5–201 Frequencies measured across the band Two-tone test Setting Console name Default Values What it does Two-tone time TTDUR 30 5–120 The two-tone test stops by itself after this many seconds Microphone for this browser Setting Console name Default Values What it does Browser mic level WEBTXLVL 100 0–200 Gain for a browser microphone, % FM Setting Console name Default Values What it does CTCSS transmit CTCSS_TX OFF OFF or one of the 38 standard tones (67.0–250.3 Hz) Tone sent with FM, for repeaters that need one"},{"p":"Troubleshooting.html","t":"Troubleshooting","h":"Troubleshooting","a":"","x":"Troubleshooting"},{"p":"Troubleshooting.html","t":"Troubleshooting","h":"The screen","a":"the-screen","x":"The browser warns about the certificate. The radio makes its own HTTPS certificate. Choose Advanced → Proceed once on each device. After changing the radio's address, delete ~/jeSDR/data/jesdr_cert.pem and jesdr_key.pem and restart to make a new one. The page doesn't load at all. Check the service on the radio: sudo systemctl status jesdr journalctl -u jesdr -f More commands, such as stopping, restarting and starting at boot, are in Managing the jeSDR service . Nothing else may use the audio codec or the GPIO: remove any other radio program from the desktop autostart. ERR_TOO_MANY_RETRIES or \"This site can't be reached\" on some phones or computers, while others work. The device isn't letting the browser reach devices on your home network. Newer Android versions, iPhones, iPads and Macs ask for this permission per app; without it, Chrome is blocked before it reaches the radio, retries, and gives up. Nothing is wrong with the radio. Allow it on the device, then reload the page: Device Where Android Settings → Apps → Chrome → Permissions → Nearby devices (on some versions Local network ) → Allow iPhone, iPad Settings → Chrome → turn on Local Network (Safari doesn't need it) Mac (macOS 15 or later) System Settings → Privacy & Security → Local Network → turn on Chrome Other browsers built on Chrome (Edge, Brave, Opera) have their own switch in the same place. Quick check: if Firefox on the same device opens the radio, it is this permission. If a device still fails, record what Chrome does: open chrome://net-export on it, start logging, load the radio's page, stop, and send the saved file along when you ask for help. The page says jeSDR is stopped. Someone used Stop jeSDR . Open jeSDR from the radio's desktop or menu, run sudo systemctl start jesdr , or restart the Pi. The desktop shortcut asks whether to execute or open. Update to the latest version; the package fixes the shortcut. No sound in the browser on a phone or laptop. Browsers start sound only after a tap: tap the 🔈 button next to PTT. It is hidden on the radio's own screen, which uses the speaker."},{"p":"Troubleshooting.html","t":"Troubleshooting","h":"Receiving","a":"receiving","x":"The waterfall turns solid or strong signals distort. The receiver is overloaded: lower the IF gain, use the attenuator, or turn on Auto IF gain . Weak signals are lost in the noise on 10–15 m. Raise the IF gain or use the preamp until the band noise moves the S-meter a little. Clicks or dropouts in the speaker. These are audio underruns. The console's status shows how many there have been since start, and the log gets one line a minute while they happen ( journalctl -u jesdr ). Check the Pi isn't hot or short of power ( More → Power & battery ), and close other heavy programs on the Pi. A steady tone sits on every signal. Try the birdie shift , or the auto notch for a tone from another station. No country under calls. Install the country file ."},{"p":"Troubleshooting.html","t":"Troubleshooting","h":"Transmitting","a":"transmitting","x":"If the radio won't transmit, the header usually says why: Shown Cause Fix TX blocked outside the transmit band plan tune inside the band, or check Band plan SWR trip SWR above the limit fix the antenna, then Clear trip on Transmit and set the drive back timeout badge the transmit timeout un-keyed release PTT; stop the program holding it battery flat battery protection charge the battery — Mic PTT is OFF turn it on in More → Transmit — the PA calibration is running wait, or Stop it Power differs from band to band. Run the PA power calibration . It keys, but little or no power comes out. See No RF output : the settings to check first, the self-tests that find most faults, and the PA chain step by step. Changed the Pi's language and lost power? That was a bug in the earlier sBitx software (a different decimal separator broke its calibration). jeSDR always reads its numbers the same way, whatever the Pi's language. A phone or laptop can't use its microphone. Open the radio over https://<radio>:8443/ , not http:// , and allow the microphone."},{"p":"Troubleshooting.html","t":"Troubleshooting","h":"Digital modes","a":"digital-modes","x":"FT8 decodes nothing, or DT is large. The clock must be right within about a second. Without internet, use a GPS . If decodes show DT around ±0.1 s after a GPS clock set, adjust GPS delay . WSJT-X can't control the radio. Rig Hamlib NET rigctl , server 127.0.0.1:4532 , PTT via CAT; see Connecting other programs . The JS8 panel says \"not connected\". JS8Call must be running ( Open JS8Call , or the menu) with its API on: Settings → Reporting → API , Enable TCP Server API and Accept TCP Requests , port 2442 (or set JS8Call API , JS8_API , to its port). If it connects and drops every few seconds, another program is using JS8Call's API: raise Max connections in JS8Call. Open JS8Call needs someone logged in to the radio's desktop. If it says JS8Call is not installed, run the installer again and answer yes to JS8Call. JS8Call hears nothing or sends no audio: choose jeSDR Receive and jeSDR Transmit in its Settings → Audio , as for WSJT-X. WSJT-X (or another program) does not list jeSDR Receive / jeSDR Transmit. status on the console says programs' audio: PipeWire … when jeSDR made them; waiting for the user's PipeWire session means the radio user's PipeWire is not running: log in to the desktop once, or run sudo loginctl enable-linger <user> and reboot. Restart the program after jeSDR started: some list audio devices only when they start. The desktop's sound goes to the transmitter. jeSDR Transmit was chosen as the desktop's output: pick jeSDR Speaker in the volume menu. WSJT-X, JS8Call or fldigi hear nothing. Their audio input must be jeSDR Receive (or the default input, which is jeSDR Receive ). status on the console shows jeSDR Receive in use while the program listens. An earlier build sent silence to jeSDR Receive : update jeSDR. No sound from SDROxide (or another program) on the radio's speaker. The program must play to jeSDR Speaker : choose it as the program's audio output or in the desktop's volume menu (the desktop keeps an output you picked before). status on the console shows jeSDR Speaker in use while it plays. The radio's volume knob sets its level. No DX cluster spots. Set My call sign , turn Cluster connection ON in More → Station , and check DX cluster spots on the scope in More → Display ."},{"p":"Troubleshooting.html","t":"Troubleshooting","h":"The Pi","a":"the-pi","x":"A red Pi badge. The Pi is hot or short of power: check More → Power & battery . Under-voltage can corrupt the SD card; use a supply that holds the voltage while transmitting. The Pi's CPU is near 100 %. jeSDR itself uses about a quarter of one core; most of the load is what draws and sends the screen. In htop : chromium (the jeSDR window on the Pi's screen): lower More → Display → Waterfall speed on that screen (10 is the default there, 5 halves it again). Open jeSDR with its menu entry or desktop icon, which turns off Chromium's forced accessibility mode. (Green lines in htop are threads of the process above them, not more copies.) wayvnc (only while a VNC viewer is connected): VNC has to encode the moving waterfall all the time. Operate from the other computer's browser instead ( https://<radio>:8443/ ): the Pi then only sends the data, and the other computer draws it. labwc (the desktop) redraws with the windows above it. No keying, no filter changes, or dead knobs. Look at the start of the log ( journalctl -u jesdr -b ) for a gpio: line: Log says Fix no permission for /dev/gpiochip* sudo usermod -aG gpio <user> , then restart GPIO8 is in use by the kernel (or 7, 9, 10, 11) SPI is on: comment out dtparam=spi=on in /boot/firmware/config.txt and restart GPIO4 is in use by the kernel a 1-wire overlay ( dtoverlay=w1-gpio ) holds the PTT pin: remove it in use by ... a second jesdr? another radio program has the pins; stop it jeSDR stops at start-up with an i2c: message. The radio's I2C bus (the Si5351, the SWR bridge, the INA260, the RTC) is the kernel's i2c-gpio bus on GPIO 13 and 6; jeSDR no longer bit-bangs those pins and can't run without it. Log says Fix the radio's I2C bus was not found add dtoverlay=i2c-rtc-gpio,ds1307,i2c_gpio_sda=13,i2c_gpio_scl=6 to /boot/firmware/config.txt and restart the Pi has no /dev/i2c-N: the i2c-dev module is not loaded sudo modprobe i2c-dev ; reinstalling the jeSDR package makes it load at every boot no permission for /dev/i2c-* sudo usermod -aG i2c <user> , then restart the Si5351 (0x60) does not answer the overlay is on other pins, or the bus is wired wrong: check the line above No RTC since the I2C change (the clock tool says \"RTC: none found\"). /boot/firmware/config.txt must have exactly one I2C overlay on GPIO 13 and 6, and it must be the clock's: dtoverlay=i2c-rtc-gpio,ds1307,i2c_gpio_sda=13,i2c_gpio_scl=6 . A plain dtoverlay=i2c-gpio,bus=3,... line runs the radio but has no clock, and both lines together hide it. Install the current jeSDR package (it fixes the line) or edit it yourself, then restart the Pi. ls /sys/class/rtc should then list a clock whose name says rtc-ds1307 . Starting again. Make a backup , write a fresh SD card image, then restore it."},{"p":"Tuning-knobs-and-MIDI.html","t":"Tuning, knobs and MIDI","h":"Tuning, knobs and MIDI","a":"","x":"Tuning, knobs and MIDI"},{"p":"Tuning-knobs-and-MIDI.html","t":"Tuning, knobs and MIDI","h":"Tuning","a":"tuning","x":"Tuning step (10 Hz, 100 Hz, 500 Hz, 1 kHz, 10 kHz) is used by the knob, the scroll wheel, the arrow keys and Hamlib's UP/DOWN. VFO lock stops the dial moving from the knob, the scope and the keyboard. Typed frequencies and CAT still tune. On a keyboard, the arrow keys tune by the step."},{"p":"Tuning-knobs-and-MIDI.html","t":"Tuning, knobs and MIDI","h":"The radio's knobs","a":"the-radios-knobs","x":"Left knob: the speaker volume; push mutes. Right knob: tunes in steps; push cycles the step; hold 1 s toggles the VFO lock. Tap a control-strip tile and the right knob adjusts that tile instead, until you push it or 10 s pass. With a TCI client connected the right knob moves the client's VFO; with an HPSDR client it is ignored (HPSDR cannot report a retune to the client). Tuning knob and Volume knob turn each knob off. Knobs AUTO sends the tuning knob to MIDI while a program is connected to the radio's MIDI port, and tunes locally otherwise."},{"p":"Tuning-knobs-and-MIDI.html","t":"Tuning, knobs and MIDI","h":"MIDI","a":"midi","x":"The radio publishes the ALSA MIDI port jeSDR:Controls , so SDROxide and other MIDI-aware programs can use the knobs, key and PTT: Control MIDI (channel 1) Suggested use Left knob (only with Volume knob to MIDI ON) CC 16, relative 64-centered Volume Right knob CC 17, relative 64-centered Tune Left knob push (with Volume knob to MIDI ) Note 60 Mute Right knob push Note 61 anything, e.g. RIT Radio transmitting (key or mic) Note 62 PTT, button mode hold Key dash / straight line Note 64 CW straight key, with Keyer OFF Key dot line Note 65 — Mic PTT line Note 66 — In SDROxide: Settings → MIDI , choose the port, then MIDI LEARN each row. For SDROxide on another computer, install rtpmidid on the Pi and connect the port with aconnect 'jeSDR:Controls' 'rtpmidid:Network' . Setting Console name Default Values What it does Tuning step STEP 100H 10H, 100H, 500H, 1K, 10K One click of the tuning knob VFO lock VFOLK OFF ON, OFF Stop the tuning knob changing frequency Hardware knobs Setting Console name Default Values What it does Knobs KNOBS AUTO AUTO, LOCAL, MIDI AUTO = MIDI while SDROxide listens Tuning knob TUNEKNOB ON ON, OFF Enable the tuning knob Volume knob VOLKNOB ON ON, OFF Enable the volume knob MIDI controller Setting Console name Default Values What it does MIDI MIDI ON ON, OFF Front panel as a MIDI controller Volume knob to MIDI VOLMIDI OFF ON, OFF Send the left knob over MIDI too"},{"p":"sBitx-hardware.html","t":"sBitx hardware and upgrades","h":"sBitx hardware and upgrades","a":"","x":"sBitx hardware and upgrades Notes on the sBitx V3 itself: its specifications, microphones, and the Raspberry Pi 5 and Touch Display 2 upgrades. To get inside, see Opening the case ."},{"p":"sBitx-hardware.html","t":"sBitx hardware and upgrades","h":"sBitx V3 specifications","a":"sbitx-v3-specifications","x":"Supply 11.5–14 V DC; full power needs 12.8–14 V at up to 9 A Receive current about 600 mA Transmit current up to 9 A at full drive Bands 80 to 10 m Typical output per band: Band 80 m 60 m 40 m 30 m 20 m 17 m 15 m 12 m 10 m Power 25 W 25 W 25 W 25 W 22 W 18 W 15 W 12 W 10 W jeSDR's PA power calibration sets the drive for each band against these figures. Use a supply that holds its voltage while transmitting: a sagging supply costs power on the high bands and can upset the Pi (see Power & battery )."},{"p":"sBitx-hardware.html","t":"sBitx hardware and upgrades","h":"Microphones","a":"microphones","x":"The stock microphone has low output and a hollow sound. Mic gain , the compressor and the transmit equalizer help, but a better microphone helps more."},{"p":"sBitx-hardware.html","t":"sBitx hardware and upgrades","h":"The mic jack","a":"the-mic-jack","x":"The sBitx mic input is a 3.5 mm TRS jack: Contact Signal Tip Mic + Ring PTT (to ground to transmit) Sleeve Ground plug base ===| GND |===| PTT |===| MIC+ |=> plug tip Few other radios wire a 3.5 mm plug this way, so another hand microphone usually needs its plug rewired or an adapter. Hand mics owners have used: TIDRadio TD-HM060 : replace its 2.5 mm TRRS plug with a 3.5 mm TRS plug. Baofeng-style speaker-microphones : rewire to a 3.5 mm TRS plug as above, and disconnect the speaker inside."},{"p":"sBitx-hardware.html","t":"sBitx hardware and upgrades","h":"A better element in the stock microphone","a":"a-better-element-in-the-stock-microphone","x":"The stock mic's electret capsule can be replaced, for example with a PUI Audio AOM-5024L-HD-R (−24 dB, noticeably more output and a clearer sound). Open the microphone. Photograph the inside first: note the capsule's wire polarity and how the PTT switch and its spring sit. Unsolder the old capsule, leaving the wires on the board. Solder the new capsule with the same polarity. Put a little loose cotton around the capsule to damp resonance. Close it up, checking the PTT spring is back in place. Then lower Mic gain to suit, and check the ALC meter ."},{"p":"sBitx-hardware.html","t":"sBitx hardware and upgrades","h":"A USB headset or microphone","a":"a-usb-headset-or-microphone","x":"A USB headset or USB sound card can be the radio's microphone, headphones or both: see Headset and USB audio ."},{"p":"sBitx-hardware.html","t":"sBitx hardware and upgrades","h":"Raspberry Pi 5","a":"raspberry-pi-5","x":"jeSDR runs on a Pi 5 as well as a Pi 4. The swap needs more than a new board: Power. The Pi 5 needs a 5 V regulator that can give 5 A ; set its output to about 5.3 V to make up for the drop in the wiring. Too little and the Pi won't start or resets. vcgencmd pmic_read_adc shows the supply rails, and jeSDR's Pi badge turns red on under-voltage. Display cable. The Pi 5's display connector is the small 22-pin type; the 7-inch display needs a Standard to Mini display cable (the Raspberry Pi 500 mm one fits the case). Cooling. The Pi 5 runs hotter: fit the Raspberry Pi Active Cooler or another fan and heatsink. jeSDR shows the Pi's temperature and warns when it is hot; keep it under about 70 °C. Case. The Ethernet and USB ports and the mounting holes are in different places, so the side panel needs new cut-outs (and perhaps new standoffs). 3D-printable replacement side plates are on the Discord server . Software. Run the one-line installer once on the Pi 5, even after writing the SD card image (the image is made on a Pi 4). Afterwards, check the display, the audio, receive and a low-power transmit into a dummy load, and look at More → Diagnostics and dmesg for errors."},{"p":"sBitx-hardware.html","t":"sBitx hardware and upgrades","h":"Touch Display 2","a":"touch-display-2","x":"The Raspberry Pi Touch Display 2 (720 × 1280) is sharper than the original 7-inch display, but it is not a drop-in replacement: It doesn't fit the old bezel. It is taller and narrower; a 3D-printable bezel is on the Discord server . Power connector. It has a different power plug: cut and solder the power wires, black to ground, brown to +5 V . Check the polarity twice. Display cable. The existing cable can be reused (on a Pi 4); insert it with the exposed contacts facing up. Software: Bring Raspberry Pi OS up to date: sudo apt update && sudo apt full-upgrade . In /boot/firmware/config.txt , make sure the display driver line is the KMS one ( dtoverlay=vc4-kms-v3d , not vc4-fkms-v3d ), and add: # Touch Display 2 dtoverlay=vc4-kms-dsi-ili9881-7inch,rotation=270 Restart. In the desktop's screen settings, turn DSI-1 to the orientation you want, and map the touchscreen ( Goodix Capacitive Touchscreen ) to it. jeSDR's screen adapts to the new size. The battery saver dims the display through the kernel's backlight control, if the display provides one."}];
